288":154,"1289":651,"1290":810,"1291":910,"1292":952,"1293":923,"1294":671,"1295":1242,"1296":828,"1297":860,"1298":661,"1299":690,"1300":1243,"1301":1144,"1302":128,"1303":798,"1304":981,"1305":977,"1306":1245,"1307":1134,"1308":706,"1309":771,"1310":296,"1311":82,"1312":81,"1313":925,"1314":136,"1315":378,"1316":509,"1317":730,"1318":762,"1319":809,"1320":861},"orderedDocs":[[50,"—"],[51,"—"],[597,"…including raw screen text (full-text search)"],[1001,"**\"About 6 min left.\"** The transcription now estimates its remaining time from its own measured pace — shown in the meetings list, on the Today page, and during a re-analysis. It appears only once there is enough real progress to be honest, and disappears rather than guess."],[1107,"**\"Add to Reminders\" works now.** Exporting your to-dos to Apple Reminders crashed the app the moment macOS asked for permission — a packaging bug, fixed and now guarded by a test. If access is denied, Hindsight explains why and offers an Open System Settings shortcut instead of failing silently."],[811,"**\"Events + focused fallback\" is gone.** The capture mode has been removed\nfrom setup and Settings. If you used it, Everpaper moves you to Adaptive\nvisual history automatically; you can pick another mode in\n**Settings → Daily history** at any time."],[1077,"**\"What did I do on my last recorded day?\" answers.** Recall always anchors on your latest recorded days and meetings, so time-based questions get an answer even when they share no words with what was on screen."],[134,"**1 minute early**"],[136,"**10 minutes early**"],[135,"**5 minutes early** (default)"],[1068,"**A calmer Today.** One heading owns the page (the date), the journal card sits inside it, Today's Focus collapses to a slim strip when there's nothing to act on, and the Ask bar and its sample question read as one composed block."],[1003,"**A checkmark means done now.** The re-analysis view used to show its checkmark when the transcription finished, while speaker separation and saving were still running."],[948,"**A clearer first run.** A new welcome explains Everpaper before setup\nstarts. The first choices separate what Everpaper remembers from the\npermissions setup will ask for, and the history step is wider, showing what\neach choice keeps and what it never records, with a summary that updates as\nyou choose."],[783,"**A fact check on the journal.** After writing, Everpaper checks the dates\nand names in the entry against what you actually did that day. If one isn't\nin the record, it asks the AI once to rewrite that part, and keeps the\nwarmth. On very full days the check may be skipped and the entry is kept as\nwritten."],[906,"**A failed read keeps the review open.** If a source can't be read while the\nday is finished, the review stays open with an error instead of showing an\nold summary."],[874,"**A failed rewrite no longer shrinks the day.** If the optional final rewrite\nfails or comes back much shorter, Everpaper keeps the day as its sources\nwrote it."],[853,"**A failed save keeps your review.** If saving the day fails, the review\nstays open so you can try again."],[953,"**A first look at Computer History.** A small evaluation build, sent out\nbetween b83 and b84, that tried the opt-in history of apps, windows, clicks\nand (only if you allowed it) encrypted typed text that b84 then shipped.\nb84 and later builds replace it."],[779,"**A launcher on ⌥Space.** Press it anywhere and type. It finds apps,\nSettings panes, commands, files (and what's inside them), images, a quick\ncalculation, and everything Everpaper remembers, with a web search when\nnothing matches."],[372,"**A little access, for a clear reason.** Your Mac asks for each permission\nseparately. Allow only what you want to use; anything you put off stays\nunavailable until you turn it on in Settings. You only see the rows your\nchoices need:"],[1252,"**A local provider keeps everything on your machine.** Connect Ollama or\nLM Studio and your text is processed locally."],[1031,"**A long transcription reports its progress to the system log.** If one ever does get stuck, there is now a record that tells a working decode apart from a stalled one."],[1027,"**A meeting being transcribed appears in the list while it works.** Until it finished, a just-stopped meeting existed nowhere — not in Meetings, not in the Library — so a long transcription looked like nothing was happening at all. It now appears immediately, carrying its own progress, and becomes the finished meeting when it is done."],[984,"**A model choice in first-run setup.** Under \"Set it up for me\", setup now names the model it will install and lets you change it. Every option shows its download size and how much memory it uses on a long meeting — not just the file size."],[1232,"**A more responsive Mac while recording.** Day Journal capture pauses for the\nduration of a meeting, so screen capture and live transcription no longer\ncompete for the Neural Engine — and it resumes automatically when the meeting\nends."],[1199,"**A new \"You\" tab.** A home for your profile: a short portrait of who you are, the traits\nit has noticed, and a timeline of how your days have felt."],[778,"**A new design.** The soft glass and warm paper of the setup pages now run\nthrough the whole app. Same places, same names, calmer to look at."],[798,"**A new permission for call audio.** Meetings now record the call's sound\nthrough macOS system audio recording, which is much lighter than screen\ncapture. Everpaper asks for the System Audio Recording permission before your\nfirst meeting, never during one. If you decline, meetings are recorded\nthrough screen capture as before, and you can ask again from Settings.\nSwitching speakers or headphones mid-meeting, Bluetooth included, no longer\nloses the recording."],[989,"**A new starter model for the one-click local AI setup.** Choosing \"set it up for me\" now installs LFM2.5 2.6B — about half the download of the previous starter, faster on long transcripts, and tuned for exactly what this app asks of it: summarizing, extracting, and answering from your own recordings."],[787,"**A new welcome and setup.** First-run setup has been redesigned to read like\nthe pages of a book: each step turns to the next, a thin ribbon marks your\nprogress, and your choices sit on soft glass over warm paper (plain paper\nbefore macOS 26, or with Reduce transparency on). The steps are the same as\nbefore, and a last page lists each part of your setup as ready, still\npreparing, or left for later."],[969,"**A one-time card on macOS 27.** If your Mac can run Everpaper Native (the\nin-app engine b80 introduced), Everpaper now tells you so once, and offers\nto set everything up: it downloads the model, switches your provider, and\nproves a first response before saying done. Decline and nothing changes;\nthe option stays in Settings."],[1225,"**A page-peel turn.** Days now turn with a subtle page-peel animation instead\nof a flat slide. Honors Reduce Motion."],[1096,"**A quieter, sharper Second Brain.** The You tab rolls websites up to the site (not every page), caps the lists, and speaks to you — no more raw tags, enum names, or numbered leftovers. Chat now owns its tab with one scroll instead of a scroll-inside-a-scroll, and duplicated model answers collapse automatically."],[1021,"**A recording interrupted mid-transcript now finishes on its own.** If Everpaper is quit or killed while it is still writing a transcript, the audio was always safe — but finishing it meant knowing to open Meetings and press Recover. Everpaper now rebuilds it the next time you open the app, and tells you it did. When it decides not to, it says so rather than leaving you to find out."],[1008,"**A recording that dies mid-meeting says so.** If the microphone drops or the disk fills, the saved meeting now says it ended early — it used to report \"Saved\" exactly like a normal stop, leaving you to discover the missing forty minutes on your own."],[362,"**A searchable memory of your work.** One page shows what Everpaper keeps:\n**Workdays you start** and **Meetings you record**, processed on this Mac by\ndefault. Press **Set up Everpaper** to begin."],[1161,"**A self-model that develops over time.** The Reflection's observations now become lasting beliefs. When the same pattern recurs across days, it firms up; when it doesn't, it fades. Recurring patterns promote to stable traits, and those traits shape what the Second Brain notices next — so its understanding deepens, not just accumulates."],[1190,"**A sidebar for the book.** An always-available table of contents. It auto-hides to give\nthe page room — hover the left edge to peek it open — or pin it from the toggle in the top-left."],[836,"**A simpler Speech page:** Everyday or Detailed, your words, and an Advanced\nsection listing the models on this Mac with their sizes and a Remove button."],[821,"**A steadier engine.** Prompts are formatted exactly as Gemma expects. An\nanswer cut off at its length limit no longer garbles the next one, and output\nmade only of internal marker tokens counts as a failure, so it never reaches\nyour journal. Structured answers on long prompts use about 1 GB of memory\ninstead of about 5.8 GB. One engine now stays loaded, closes after ten idle\nminutes, and restarts between requests if its memory grows too much."],[1132,"**A steadier Second Brain.** Beliefs no longer fade away prematurely, so your self-model and persona stay populated; chat citations resolve to the right frames after you reopen a conversation; and the self-model keeps updating even in Low Power Mode."],[831,"**A summary for meetings.** Each meeting of more than a few minutes gets a\nsummary once it ends, shown on its page and reused in the evening entry.\nLonger meetings get longer summaries. This needs an AI option set up, and a\ncloud provider needs your consent."],[375,"**Accessibility** and **Input Monitoring**: only if you turned on Computer\nHistory."],[376,"**Accessibility**, marked **OPTIONAL**, with Detailed screen history: it\nadds exact images of the focused window. The timed screenshots work without\nit."],[262,"**Activity only** (Settings: Events only)"],[369,"**Activity only**: a timeline of apps and windows, with no screenshots."],[946,"**Adaptive mode reacts to real changes.** Adaptive (today's Detailed screen\nhistory) follows the apps and windows you use, and takes an image of the\nwindow you're working in only when what you're doing changes and every\nprivacy check passes. In this build it took no full-screen screenshots. The\ntext in those images is read on this Mac."],[1217,"**AI answers are now formatted.** Responses from Ask, the Second Brain, and\nmeeting Q\\&A render their Markdown — bold, italic, headings, and bullet lists —\ninstead of showing raw \"\\*\\*\" and \"\\*\" characters."],[606,"**AI refinement of insights**"],[797,"**AI waits for the right moment.** Background AI work runs through one\nqueue, and anything you're waiting for goes first. Background jobs hold off\nwhile another app uses the microphone, while you record a meeting, in full\nscreen, when the Mac is hot or short on memory and in Low Power Mode; a\nlocked screen counts as a quiet moment, so waiting work catches up then, and\nwork with a deadline still gets done in time. Settings\nand Today say why work is paused, and **AI use today** in\n**Settings → General** shows how much each feature used the AI today."],[629,"**All meetings**: **Settings → General → Setup & data → Clear meeting\nhistory** (type DELETE to confirm). &#x2A;*Settings → Privacy → Delete saved\nhistory…** takes you there."],[585,"**Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal**"],[594,"**Allow external agents (MCP)**"],[819,"**Already on Everpaper Native with Qwen?** A one-time card offers the switch\nto Gemma and shows its sizes. Qwen keeps answering while Gemma downloads, and\nits files (about 2.3 GB) stay until you remove them."],[1179,"**Always-on sidebar.** The book's Parts are visible by default on launch; unpin from the masthead toggle if you'd rather have the room."],[1156,"**Always-visible, always-refreshable.** The Today's Focus rail is now always on screen — even when empty, it shows what to do to surface tasks. A refresh button lets you regenerate on demand."],[58,"**An AI setup you already have is kept**: Everpaper Native, Managed oMLX,\nLM Studio or Ollama."],[829,"**An evening entry in your voice.** When you finish your day, Everpaper\nwrites one warm, first-person entry from the day's notes and meetings,\nincluding each meeting's decisions, dates and who does what. A day cut short\nby a quit or crash is written up the next time Everpaper opens."],[867,"**An optional after-meeting speaker check.** A separate opt-in pass checks the\nspeaker labels once the meeting ends."],[926,"**An optional introduction.** Before setup, a short interactive introduction\nshows what Everpaper remembers and how a detail you need later becomes an\nanswer with its source. Drag through the examples or skip it."],[1115,"**Answers cite your files.** Ask the Second Brain something that lives in an indexed folder and the answer now draws on those files, with \\[file:n] citations pointing at the exact file and lines it used."],[1186,"**Answers from real content.** Ask \"why do I keep working with this client?\" and the chat reads\nthe actual text of the relevant moments — emails, docs, messages — and grounds its answer\nin them, with citations you can click to jump straight to the frame."],[929,"**Answers show what they used.** A meeting answer links to the meeting's\ntranscript and your saved notes."],[95,"**Anthropic, OpenAI, Google Gemini, OpenRouter:** nothing is sent until you\nturn on **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal** (Settings → **AI &\nsummaries** → **Your memory & summaries**). It's off by default and also\nappears in Settings → **Privacy** as **Allow online journal analysis**. Until\nyou turn it on, summaries, Memory and the other AI features wait. **Share\nyour profile with cloud AI** is a separate switch beneath it."],[46,"**Anthropic**, **OpenAI**, **Google Gemini**, **OpenRouter**"],[349,"**Apple silicon** (M-series). Everpaper's speech and AI models run on Apple\nsilicon's Neural Engine and GPU. It does not run on Intel Macs."],[490,"**As we go.** Below your notes, the As we go digest keeps track of topics (with\nminute marks), decisions, open questions and next steps. It first appears\nafter about two minutes of conversation and refreshes at most every three\nminutes. It needs an AI provider and pauses while your Mac is saving power."],[928,"**Ask a meeting in threads.** Questions about a saved meeting now live in\nconversations you can return to: a list beside the chat on wide windows, a\nmenu on narrow ones. The composer stays at the bottom, and an unsent draft\nsurvives switching conversations."],[1087,"**Ask without asking.** Pause typing for half a second and the Concordance starts recalling: an AI answer grounded in your captured days streams in right above the results. It's ephemeral — panel answers never clutter your Second Brain conversation. Press ⌘Return to continue the thought in the full app when it deserves a longer conversation."],[476,"**Audio sources**"],[1157,"**Auto-extract after meetings.** When a meeting finishes, its action items are extracted automatically — you don't have to do anything."],[876,"**Automatic Episodes and Chapters are paused.** No new ones are made for now;\nthe ones you have stay."],[885,"**Better meeting excerpts.** When a transcript has to be shortened, Everpaper\nkeeps opening questions, whole sentences and the \"but…\" that qualifies a\npoint."],[986,"**Bigger models answer properly now.** The larger Qwen options had a habit of \"thinking out loud\" — pages of reasoning instead of the answer. Setup now configures them to answer directly."],[465,"**Calendar**: Everpaper can remind you or start recording when a meeting\nbegins. See Calendar Auto-Start."],[272,"**Capture focused text (deep read)** reads the focused window's text through\nAccessibility for richer recall. It's off by default and runs through the\nsame redaction as everything else. The text is stored on this Mac, and like\nother screen text your AI can read it. Turn it on under\n**Settings → Daily history → Advanced** (Screen history mode)."],[806,"**Capture is opt-out.** Before, Everpaper took a screenshot only when it\ncould prove a window was safe. Now it takes one unless what's in front is an\napp or site you skip, or a private browsing window. Timed screenshots and\nAdaptive window shots follow the same rules, so the two can't disagree."],[1030,"**Capture that quietly fails is now visible.** A tick that captures frames but cannot store them, or a second display that stops producing frames, is reported rather than passing for healthy."],[1250,"**Capture, transcription, and storage stay local.** Meetings are transcribed\non your Mac with the on-device speech model (Parakeet, which runs on the Neural\nEngine), and your journal database never leaves it."],[436,"**Capture, transcription, and storage stay local.** Meetings are transcribed\non your Mac, and your journal database never leaves it."],[886,"**Changes ripple correctly.** When a source changes or is deleted, the\nsummaries built on it are refreshed together, and your own notes and titles\nare kept."],[1312,"**Check and download**: a found update downloads in the background, ready to\ninstall."],[1311,"**Check only** (default): Everpaper checks once a day and asks before\ndownloading."],[64,"**Choose advanced options** shows every route: **Everpaper Native** (on\nmacOS 27), **Managed oMLX**, any local servers it found (with **Connect**),\n**Use my own provider** (LM Studio, Ollama, Nativ, a custom endpoint or a cloud\nprovider; finish with **Test & Use**), and **No AI for now**."],[1126,"**Choose your embedding model — and it's used everywhere.** The embedding model you pick for Folder Context RAG now drives *both* indexing and search, so your files and your queries are always embedded with the same model (a mismatch used to silently break semantic search). Point it at a local model in Ollama, LM Studio, or oMLX and nothing leaves your Mac; a cloud embedding model only ever receives your file text if you explicitly choose one and grant consent."],[1072,"**Cleaner previews.** Archive cards preview their transcript in the reading face instead of monospace, and the feature notes on Today read as quiet prose instead of a settings list."],[1150,"**Cleaner, more useful todos.** Citation markers and raw URLs/IPs no longer leak into your todo titles, and machine-internal detail text is replaced with human guidance. A tighter, higher-quality list."],[817,"**Clear sizes, careful switch.** The download is about 5.9 GB, and the\ninstalled model takes about 8.5 GB (some tools show 7.9 GB). Setup checks\nthere's room for both before it starts, and switches over only after Gemma\nhas answered a first request. If the connection drops for a moment, the\ndownload retries and carries on where it stopped."],[833,"**Clearer live speaker labels.** A new caption line starts when the speaker\nchanges; in our tests, words shown under the wrong speaker fell from about\n31% to about 10%. When several people speak into your Mac's own microphone,\nthe saved transcript separates them, and you stay \"Me\" when you're the only\none talking."],[1205,"**Clearer meetings.** The section is consistently called \"Meetings\", larger calls\nget more distinct speaker colors, the connect-calendar prompt refreshes after you\ngrant access, and a failed prompt now shows an error instead of doing nothing."],[1234,"**Clearer Screen Recording setup.** When Hindsight is opened from outside your\nApplications folder — where macOS runs it from a temporary, locked-down copy —\nsetup now tells you to move Hindsight to Applications and reopen it, which is\nthe only way the Screen Recording permission will stick. Previously it pointed\nyou at the \"+\" button, which can't take effect in that case."],[851,"**Click details are read safely.** When Computer History notes what you\nclicked, it now reads that detail the way macOS expects. Reading it in the\nbackground could trip an error inside Everpaper's own windows, and was linked\nto a crash and a hang in b94."],[497,"**Click** to open the panel while recording; after you stop, a click shows\nthe transcription progress or opens the saved meeting."],[371,"**Clicks, selections, and typed text** (beta; any mode except Screen history).\nOff until you turn on **Computer History events & text**. When it's on,\nEverpaper remembers clicks, shortcuts, selected text and typed text in apps you\nhaven't excluded, which can include email and messages. Typed text is kept as\nan encrypted, redacted record, and the original text expires after 48 hours.\nPassword fields and private browsing are always excluded. Everything is stored\non this Mac. Your AI reads it to write your day, and a cloud AI needs a\nseparate consent. This opt-in also asks\nfor **Accessibility** and **Input Monitoring** in the next step. See\nComputer History."],[434,"**Clicks, selections, and typed text** (Computer History): beta, off until\nyou turn it on. See Privacy."],[780,"**Clipboard history.** The launcher also keeps what you copy, so you can\npaste something from earlier. It is on by default, keeps copies for 7 days,\nstays on this Mac, and skips passwords that apps mark as secret, private\nwindows it can detect, and the apps and sites you skip. Everpaper tells you\nonce and lets you turn it off on the spot, or later in Settings."],[439,"**Cloud is opt-in.** Nothing goes to a cloud AI until you connect one and\nturn on **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal** (Settings → AI &\nsummaries; in Settings → Privacy it's called **Allow online journal\nanalysis**). The same switch covers journal text,\nmeeting transcripts, questions and notes. Before anything is sent, secrets\nsuch as keys and card numbers, and contact details such as email addresses\nand phone numbers, are redacted."],[1253,"**Cloud is opt-in.** Sending anything to a cloud provider is off until you\nturn it on, and the same gate applies uniformly to journal text, meeting\ntranscripts, chat, and drafts."],[555,"**Cloud providers are gated by consent.** A cloud provider (Anthropic, OpenAI,\nGoogle Gemini, OpenRouter) is only allowed to receive your data after you turn\non **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal**, which is **off by default**."],[1104,"**Cold models are waited for, not skipped.** If your local AI server is still loading a model when indexing starts, Hindsight now waits for it — retrying for a couple of minutes with a \"Waiting for the embedding model to load…\" status — instead of silently falling back to the weaker built-in embedder for the whole run. Meaning-aware chunking gets the same fix: a model that takes more than a minute to load no longer loses semantic splitting for every file."],[668,"**Commands**: actions such as Turn to today, Begin meeting / End meeting,\nRun setup again, Check for updates."],[930,"**Commitments open in place.** Clicking a commitment opens a small popover\nbeside it with its title, status and source. Escape closes the source first,\nthen the popover."],[665,"**Commonplace Book**: beliefs Memory has formed."],[966,"**Completed meetings no longer leave a stale progress row.** The meeting list\nreconciles its live finalization state against the saved record after every\nrefresh."],[945,"**Computer History (tester builds only).** An opt-in history of what you do\non your Mac: apps and windows, clicks and shortcuts, and — only if you allow\nit — encrypted typed text. Password fields and private browsing are never\nrecorded, you can exclude apps and sites, and it's stored only on this Mac."],[1082,"**Concordance actually finds your meetings now.** Asking \"what did I talk about in my last meeting?\" used to come up empty — the AI answer only ever looked at your Days, never at meeting transcripts. It now grounds on meetings and indexed files too, with real semantic search over what was said, not just literal keyword matches."],[1053,"**Connect your intelligence during setup.** A new step finds a local AI server already running on your Mac (Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX) and connects it in one click — no key, no cloud, nothing leaves your machine. Recall, the Second Brain, and meeting prompts work from your very first day instead of waiting for a trip to Settings."],[271,"**Context.** The app and window in front, so activities are easy to make\nsense of later."],[912,"**Custom words guide the standard engine.** Words you add now act as gentle\nhints for standard transcription, so names and terms are more likely to come\nout right. With an empty list nothing changes."],[1224,"**Day navigation works again.** The left/right arrow keys (and ⌘\\[ / ⌘]) turn\nbetween days without having to click the page first, and the optional\ntwo-finger swipe (Colophon → \"Turn pages with a two-finger swipe\") is reliable."],[1098,"**Days that read like a day.** Timeline entries describe what was actually on screen (never keyword soup), quick message-checking hops fold into one \"Checking messages\" row, and a new MARGINALIA column surfaces the day's open questions and loose ends — only when there are any, and never invented."],[312,"**Days/**: one note per captured day, with the day's narrative, a timeline of\nwhat you worked on, and linked lists of the **People**, **Links**, and **Files**\nseen that day. Your own notes for the day come along too. Each note carries\nfrontmatter (`date`, `captures`, `tags`)."],[20,"**Decisions** and **Commitments** on a day page"],[1007,"**Deleting a meeting can no longer half-delete it.** The audio was removed before the database entry, so a delete that failed partway left a meeting you could still see but never play. The order is reversed, and a failure now says so instead of pretending nothing happened."],[1028,"**Described for what it is.** That entry reads \"Transcribing\", not \"Recovering\" — recovery wording is kept for a recording genuinely orphaned by a crash, so it still means something when you see it."],[938,"**Detailed screen history can't record less than Screen history.** It keeps\nthe regular full-screen snapshots, then adds a closer look at the active\nwindow after real changes, pauses in typing, or a Moment you mark. Without\nAccessibility permission, only those extras stop."],[259,"**Detailed screen history** (Settings: Adaptive visual history)"],[368,"**Detailed screen history**: Screen history plus extra images of the window\nyou're focused on."],[1136,"**Diagnostic logging.** The embeddings path now logs to Console.app (subsystem `com.iannellomarco.hindsight.core`, category `Embeddings` + `FileIndexer`). You can see exactly what model was requested, whether it succeeded, and when the app fell back to the on-device model — filter Console by the subsystem and send the logs when something's off."],[781,"**Dictate on fn.** Hold fn, speak, let go, and your words land where the\ncursor is. It works in Italian and English, and a sentence that mixes the two\nstays as you said it: the clean-up no longer translates the smaller\nlanguage. It runs on this Mac with the speech model you already have, never\ndownloads anything on its own, and is off until you turn it on, in setup or\nin **Settings → Dictation**. Turning it on asks for the microphone."],[493,"**Discard.** The trash icon next to **Stop recording** asks &#x2A;Discard\nRecording?* before throwing away everything captured so far."],[1194,"**Dismiss sticks.** When you dismiss something the Second Brain inferred about you, it\nno longer quietly comes back the next day."],[1109,"**Dismissed means dismissed.** To-dos you dismiss, complete, or export stay gone across relaunches — and a suppression quietly expires after a month, so a recurring task with the same name isn't hidden forever."],[1282,"**Does this fit?** asks you to confirm details Everpaper inferred, such as your\nrole (**Yes, that's me** / **Not me**). **About you** shows what you wrote in\n**Settings → AI & summaries → Your memory & summaries → About you**. **Who you\nare** is a short portrait your AI model writes from your history; **Refresh**\nwrites it again. **What I'm picking up about you** lists traits drawn from your\nrhythms, the tools you rely on and observations that keep recurring. Confirm\none with ✓ or remove it with ✕. **How your days have felt** charts the mood of\nrecent days."],[480,"**Double-check speakers by voice**"],[1048,"**Download & Set Up — and that's it.** If no local AI server is running, first-time setup now offers to do everything: it installs oMLX (a free, notarized inference server built for Apple Silicon), downloads the Qwen3.5 4B model (\\~4 GB) from Hugging Face with live progress, starts the server, and connects Hindsight the moment the model is ready. No account, no API key, no terminal — and nothing ever leaves your Mac."],[631,"**Downloaded models**: speech models under **Settings → Speech → Models on\nthis Mac → Remove**; Everpaper Native models under **Settings → AI &\nsummaries → AI connection → Remove**."],[352,"**Downloads.** Nothing downloads during setup until you press **Prepare on\nthis Mac**. After setup, the search model sets itself up in the background\nat a quiet moment. **Set up later** or **Cancel preparation** puts that off\nfor a week. See Search by meaning."],[1302,"**During the private beta, Everpaper does not update itself.** Each new beta\narrives as a private download link. Install it over your current copy, as\nbelow. Automatic updates start with the first public release."],[901,"**Each update reads its own part of the day.** In recent betas every journal\nupdate could reuse the day's first minutes, so the journal stopped moving\nearly in the day. Each update now reads the stretch of time it covers."],[1099,"**Easier on the eyes.** Long AI answers now read in the book's serif with proper line spacing instead of typewriter-style text, numbered lists align properly everywhere, and the Archive gained date groups, clickable favorite stars, a clearer filter bar, and word-by-word search."],[807,"**Email and chat are no longer skipped on their own.** Adaptive window shots\nused to leave out email, chat and messaging apps and sites by themselves. If\nyou want them kept out, add them to your skip lists; **Skip sites** offers\nthem as one-tap suggestions."],[678,"**Emails**"],[830,"**Entries can outlive their screenshots.** With **Also delete their text &\nrecall data** off (Settings → Daily history), finished entries are kept when\nold screenshots are cleaned up. The switch is on for new installs, so there a\nday's entry is removed with its screenshots after your retention period."],[140,"**Events with a call link** (default): a recognized link from Zoom, Google\nMeet, Microsoft Teams, Webex, Around or Whereby, or another link that looks\nlike a call."],[141,"**Events with guests**: at least one other person invited."],[1314,"**Everpaper → Check for Updates…** in the app menu, or the same item in the\nEverpaper menu-bar icon"],[34,"**Everpaper Native (macOS 27)**"],[964,"**Everpaper Native leads on macOS 27.** The built-in engine is now the\nrecommended intelligence choice during setup; a separate local engine remains\navailable as an alternative. System instructions also use the model's real\nprivileged system role."],[60,"**Everpaper Native with Gemma 4 E4B**, on macOS 27 with 16 GB of memory or\nmore."],[92,"**Everpaper Native:** runs inside the app."],[380,"**Everpaper Native** on macOS 27: Gemma 4 E4B with 16 GB of memory or more,\nQwen3 4B below that."],[828,"**Everpaper now needs macOS 15 (Sequoia) or later.**"],[1166,"**Every answer is now verified.** The Second Brain's honesty guard — which strips any claim it can't ground in your actual captured data — now runs on every chat answer, not just the relational ones."],[142,"**Every event**: every timed event."],[1000,"**Every step has a name.** Preparing the audio, transcribing, separating speakers, saving — the pill, the meetings row, and the Today page now say which one is running. Before this build, only the transcription itself reported anything, so the bar could sit at 100% for minutes while real work happened in silence."],[958,"**Every usable route stays visible.** Everpaper Native leads on macOS 27,\nwith Managed oMLX, detected local providers, your own endpoint, and no AI\nall available from the same screen. The redundant Apple Intelligence row is\ngone; its icon now identifies Everpaper Native."],[832,"**Everyday meetings: Parakeet for words, Nemotron for speakers,** live and in\nthe saved transcript. If Nemotron can't run, Everpaper falls back to its\nprevious speaker labels."],[865,"**Everyday or Detailed meetings.** Settings → Speech now opens with two\nchoices, explained by purpose and speed. The exact models are listed under\n\"What runs on my Mac\"."],[959,"**Existing local setups are reused.** Setup can rescan providers, preserves\nthe chosen route through the final receipt, and reuses installed oMLX and\nmodel files instead of downloading them again."],[1036,"**Existing users get one quiet announcement.** On first launch after updating, a single \"New in this impression\" notice explains the model and offers to set it up in place, with live download progress. Dismiss it and it never returns — a Press Check line and the new Meeting Model entry under Settings → Transcription & Speech remain the permanent path."],[1121,"**Export any transcript.** Save a meeting's transcript to a file — plain text, Markdown, subtitles (SRT or VTT), or JSON — from the new Export button on the meeting, or \"Save Transcript…\" in the Library's right-click menu. Everything is written locally to a file you choose; nothing is uploaded."],[319,"**Export graph (GraphML + Mermaid)** writes your knowledge graph — the people,\nsites, and files that recur together — in two visualization-ready formats:"],[309,"**Export Obsidian vault** writes your whole journal as a folder of linked\nMarkdown notes — the kind of vault Obsidian, Logseq, or any plain-Markdown editor\ncan open directly."],[1288,"**Export** (⌥⌘6) writes files on your Mac; nothing is uploaded.\n**Export Obsidian vault** writes one note per day, with its written entry, your\nnotes, a timeline, and the people, sites and files seen that day. &#x2A;*Export\ngraph (GraphML + Mermaid)** writes your connections as a graph file you can\nopen in yEd or Gephi, plus a Mermaid diagram for Markdown. **Reveal in Finder**\nopens the folder. See Export for where the files go and how to use\nthem."],[177,"**Export** supports several formats with `--format`: `txt` (default),\n`markdown`, `srt`, `vtt`, and `json`. By default the file is written as\n`<title>.<ext>` in the current directory; use `--output <path>` to choose the\nname, or `--stdout` to print instead of writing a file."],[101,"**External embeddings provider** (advanced, optional) points file indexing at\na server you run instead: Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX or an OpenAI-compatible\nendpoint. It's separate from your chat AI, and the same rules about where text\ngoes apply to it. With none set, files use the search model."],[1092,"**Extract To-Dos works on any meeting.** Extracting to-dos from a transcript silently did nothing for meetings older than three days — a Today-rail recency rule had leaked into the explicit action. It now works regardless of age, tells you the outcome (\"3 to-dos added to Today.\" or an honest none-found note), and re-extracting resurfaces to-dos you'd previously exported or dismissed instead of silently skipping them."],[1154,"**Extract Todos from any meeting.** A new checklist button in the meeting detail reads the transcript and pulls out the action items — things you said you'd do, were asked to do, or agreed to. They land in Today's Focus immediately, each tagged with the meeting it came from."],[1211,"**Far better speaker separation.** A one-on-one call is no longer split into a\nhandful of phantom speakers, and genuine group meetings keep everyone — the\nspeaker count is no longer capped, so a large call isn't truncated down to a\nfew voices."],[1131,"**Faster, more honest day pages.** Opening a day no longer hitches the interface, screenshots use far less memory, the timeline shows the whole day (not just the first stretch of it), per-app time and on-disk storage are reported accurately, and deleting a day takes a single confirmation."],[939,"**Fewer near-identical screenshots.** Routine snapshots are smaller, and\nframes that look the same as the last one are dropped before anything is\nstored or read. Moments you mark keep full quality."],[1141,"**File content never leaves your Mac.** The fallback path that used your chat AI for embeddings is now gated to local providers only — a cloud chat provider will never receive your file text for embedding."],[1114,"**File indexing is fast now.** Folders index in parallel waves instead of one file at a time, re-indexing an unchanged folder finishes in seconds with zero wasted work, and a single huge file can no longer stall the run. On machines running a local AI, indexing also stops swapping models back and forth for every file — the biggest source of slowdown."],[1129,"**File search actually uses your vectors.** Semantic file search now runs whenever you've typed a query (multi-word queries used to silently drop to keyword-only), re-indexes only the files that changed instead of re-embedding the whole folder every time, can be cancelled mid-run, and tells you when a folder has nothing to index."],[790,"**Files found by meaning.** In the Concordance, the Files list (and the file\nrows in Everything) shows keyword matches at once, then adds the files that\nmatch what you meant a moment later."],[680,"**Files**"],[315,"**Files/**: one note per file, backlinked to the days it appeared."],[1061,"**Find any setting, in place.** A permanent \"Find any setting…\" field lives in the pinned header (⌘F). Typing filters the page to matching settings — synonyms included, so \"mic\" finds Audio Input — and choosing one lands right on the row. Esc restores the page."],[1056,"**Finish by starting.** The last page ends with a real \"Start Journaling\" press that begins today's record on the spot, plus an optional \"Open Hindsight at login\" so the daybook is running on the days that matter. And if macOS asks for a relaunch mid-setup, you now resume right where you left off instead of starting over."],[1237,"**Fixed a launch crash on some Macs.** The bundled Daybook fonts failed to\nload on Macs other than the one the app was built on, which could crash\nHindsight at startup. Fonts now load reliably on every Mac."],[784,"**Fixes.** A dictation on a day without a journal now shows up in the\nhistory. A crash in the on-device sentence model is fixed. A journal digest\nthat retried for hours now gives up cleanly. Dates are no longer read into\nordinary words such as \"set 5 reminders\". Setup options answer VoiceOver. On\nthe screen: the ribbon sits in the masthead, Today's last line stays\nvisible, the progress track is readable in dark mode, and the related-moments\nswitch moved to **Daily history**."],[1076,"**Follow up without re-asking.** Answers in the Concordance now build into a conversation: type the next question and the panel carries the thread with it. ⌘↵ still promotes the exchange into the Second Brain — now with its context attached, so a follow-up like \"did we set a deadline?\" survives the jump."],[350,"**For Everpaper Native, the built-in private AI: macOS 27.** With 16 GB of\nmemory or more it runs Gemma 4 E4B; with less, Qwen3 4B. On macOS 15 and 26,\nsetup installs a managed oMLX model instead (Gemma 4 E4B from 24 GB of\nmemory, a smaller model below that), or uses an LM Studio or Ollama you\nalready run. See AI on your Mac."],[1040,"**For the terminal.** `hindsight-cli transcribe --engine moss --diarize` runs the same joint model on any audio file."],[351,"**Free disk space for the on-device models:** about 1 GB for meeting\ntranscription, plus about 2 to 9 GB for the private AI model, depending on\nyour Mac. Gemma 4 E4B in Everpaper Native needs about 16 GB free while it\ninstalls and about 8.5 GB afterwards. The search model adds about 1.5 GB\non macOS 27, and less on earlier macOS."],[1111,"**Fresher suggestions.** Meeting action items older than a week stop resurfacing on Today as if they were new."],[509,"**from the calendar invite**: a one-to-one call whose invite lists exactly\none guest."],[835,"**Gemma 4 E4B for Macs with 24 GB or more** (about 6.9 GB). Setup reuses an\nLM Studio or Ollama you already run instead of installing a second engine.\nThinking is turned off, every downloaded file is checked, and existing setups\nare kept."],[816,"**Gemma 4 inside Everpaper Native.** On macOS 27 Macs with 16 GB of memory\nor more, Everpaper Native now runs Gemma 4 E4B, the model that wrote the best\nevening entries in our tests. It runs inside Everpaper, so you don't need\nLM Studio, Ollama or any other server."],[320,"**graph.graphml**: open it in yEd,\nGephi, or Obsidian's graph plugins for an interactive,\nlaid-out view."],[321,"**graph.mermaid**: a Mermaid `graph TD` diagram you\ncan paste straight into Markdown, Obsidian, or Notion, or render at\nmermaid.live."],[317,"**GRAPH\\_REPORT.md**: an overview of the vault: your most-connected\nnodes, surprising connections, and a few questions worth exploring, all\nresolving to the notes above."],[513,"**Hide from transcript**: on the **Speakers** tab, hide any speaker. Hidden\nspeakers appear in a **Hidden** section with **Unhide** and **Unhide all**."],[512,"**Hide my voice from this transcript**: a link at the top of the transcript\nremoves your turns from view; **Show my voice** brings them back."],[1164,"**Hide your voice from transcripts.** A one-click \"Hide my voice\" toggle on any meeting transcript. Your turns disappear from the view, but nothing is destroyed — bring them back anytime. It survives re-analysis too."],[947,"**History reads as work.** A day's History now leads with Episodes: short\nwrite-ups of what you worked on, each with a title, the apps involved, how\nlong it took and any Moments you marked. The raw list of app switches and\nclicks is still there underneath, as detail."],[877,"**History shows spans you can open.** Recorded activities are grouped into\nstretches of time, each opening its sources."],[949,"**Honest about thin days.** Vague “Moving between…” rows are replaced with\nwhat was actually seen or typed, when that's available. When there's too\nlittle to go on, Everpaper says so instead of inventing a task or outcome."],[1183,"**Honest about what vision sees.** The vision setting and the on-day redaction badge now say plainly: captured text is scrubbed, but visible pixels sent for descriptions are not."],[1039,"**Honest edges.** Word-level timestamps inside a sentence are approximate with MOSS (segment boundaries are exact), and a voice that stays silent for a very long stretch may re-enter under a new speaker label. Raw and Clean Lexicon modes apply to MOSS transcripts exactly as they do everywhere else."],[462,"**Hotkey**: press ⇧⌘M from any app; press it again to stop.\nChange it in **Settings → Meetings → Meeting hotkey**."],[496,"**Hover** to show Pause and a separate Stop button."],[366,"**How should your day be remembered?** This is what Everpaper may observe while\na workday journal is running:"],[571,"**How typed text is kept.** As an encrypted, redacted record. The key is kept\nin this Mac's Keychain and doesn't sync. The original text is gone after 48\nhours."],[1285,"**How your picture has changed**: how Memory's understanding of you has\nshifted over time."],[1083,"**Import a transcript, get a meeting.** Have a .vtt transcript from Zoom, Teams, or Otter but no Hindsight recording? Meetings → Import Transcript… turns it into a real meeting — searchable and recallable just like one you recorded, no audio required."],[464,"**Index**: press ⌘K and choose **Begin meeting** (it reads **End\nmeeting** while you're recording)."],[1103,"**Indexing shows its work again.** The progress bar appears the moment indexing starts — no more long silent stretch on a fresh index — and a live status line tells you exactly what's happening: \"Reading files…\", \"Splitting files by meaning…\", \"Embedding chunks (batch 2/7)…\". Pause and Cancel are always visible while a run is active, and pausing now takes effect mid-run instead of waiting for the current batch of files to finish."],[543,"**Insights, Connections and beliefs** in Memory are worked out on this Mac\nfrom your own history, so they work with no AI provider connected at all."],[1049,"**Interruption-proof.** A dropped connection doesn't restart the 4 GB download — setup resumes from the files that already finished. If oMLX's own first-run screen holds things up, Hindsight starts the server directly so setup still completes on its own."],[1022,"**It gives each recording one automatic try.** If rebuilding a particular recording is what stopped the app, Everpaper will not keep walking into that at every launch — that one waits for you to choose, and everything else still rebuilds on its own."],[1197,"**It knows you.** Tell it your name and role (during setup, or anytime in Settings) and\nit speaks to you personally — greeting you by name and writing your day as someone it\nactually knows."],[1187,"**It knows you're not your own coworker.** The Second Brain no longer lists you among the\npeople you work with."],[1198,"**It learns you.** From your own days it quietly works out things like your role, the\nway you work, and how you've been feeling — and asks you to confirm the important guesses\nbefore trusting them. Nothing is assumed, and anything that feels off is one tap to dismiss."],[1057,"**It moves like the book.** Steps turn like pages with a live progress rail, permissions flip to a green check with a small bounce the moment they land, and the whole flow quiets to simple fades when Reduce Motion is on."],[1023,"**It stands aside for the work in front of you.** Automatic rebuilding never begins during a meeting, stops if you start one, and holds off while your Mac is in Low Power Mode."],[782,"**Journal dates that are right.** The journal now knows today's date and the\nweek around it, so \"yesterday\", \"next Friday\" or \"5-9 Oct\" become real dates\ninstead of guesses."],[609,"**Journal threads**"],[278,"**Journal threads** (**Settings → AI & summaries → Your memory & summaries**,\noff by default) groups the day's moments by topic, picks the most telling ones\nwhen a day is too long to send in full, and lets the evening entry recall a\nfew related moments from earlier days. In testing it made the entry more\nfactual and less personal, which is why it starts off. When it's on, a second\nswitch, &#x2A;*Evening threads (experimental)**, appears beneath it."],[800,"**Journal.** Running notes build up through the day instead of being\nrewritten each time, with one full tidy-up in the evening. New and off by\ndefault, **Journal threads** in **Settings → AI & summaries** groups the day\nby topic and lets the evening entry recall related moments from earlier\ndays. In our tests it made entries more factual but less personal."],[285,"**Keep screenshots for** (**Settings → Daily history → How long it stays**)\ndeletes screenshots older than **30 days** by default (or 7, 90 days, or\nForever). In Detailed screen history the setting reads **Keep checkpoints\nfor**; in Activity only, **Keep visual captures for**.\nThe same choice is also in **Settings → Privacy → How long it stays**, as\n**Keep screenshot images for**, with the switch below under &#x2A;*Manage saved\ntext…** as **Also delete expired text & recall data**."],[1174,"**Keyboard tabs in meetings.** Switch a meeting's Transcript / Results / Chat / Speakers with `⌘1`–`⌘4`, matching the rest of the app."],[796,"**Less repeated work.** At launch, AI work waits for the first quiet moment.\nFinish day returns sooner and names the day's activities afterwards. The\nToday summary and the day's extraction aren't redone when nothing changed,\nand stopping an AI answer no longer restarts the on-device model. Old model\ncache files are cleaned up, and downloaded models are kept out of Time\nMachine backups."],[666,"**Lexicon**: your word corrections and saved phrases."],[420,"**Library**: every meeting recording and imported audio file."],[795,"**Lighter during meetings.** The recording dots, the meeting pill and the\nmeeting desk no longer pulse, the desk waveform redraws only when there's new\nsound, and spinners stop when their window is hidden. The live preview no\nlonger writes temporary audio files, and the live meeting summary reads only\nwhat was said since its last update."],[990,"**Lighter Macs get a lighter model.** On a MacBook Neo the setup installs a smaller, faster model instead, so summaries finish quickly on passive cooling."],[573,"**Limiting it.** &#x2A;*Settings → Daily history → Interaction source policy →\nManage…** sets the default for apps and websites: **Observe unless excluded**\nor **Only included sources**, plus rules per app and per website. For\nper-website rules in Safari, turn on the **Everpaper Browser Bridge** extension\n(**Safari setup** in the same sheet; pair with a code valid for five minutes).\nIt sends only the current site's address and whether the window is private,\nand only to Everpaper on this Mac. **Revoke all** removes every pairing."],[679,"**Links**"],[314,"**Links/**: one note per web address seen, backlinked to the days it\nappeared."],[866,"**Live speaker labels (experimental, opt-in).** For Everyday meetings, you\ncan turn on live speaker labels after setting up a local Nemotron model of\nabout 190 MB. People on the other end get labels during the call, which may\nchange as it goes. When the model is unsure, captions keep Me / Others. The\nwords still come from the usual live recognizer."],[478,"**Live speaker labels**"],[492,"**Live Transcript.** Captions appear a few seconds after people speak. When the\npanel is narrow, the transcript moves to a bar along the bottom. When several\npeople share your Mac's own microphone, the live transcript shows them all as\n*Me*; the saved transcript separates them."],[801,"**llama.cpp engine (experimental), off by default.** A new switch in\n**Settings → AI & summaries** runs a Gemma model on this Mac through a\ndifferent engine. It needs 16 GB of memory or more and downloads its model\nthe first time you turn it on. Turning it off returns to the AI option you\nhad before."],[915,"**LM Studio answers straight away with Qwen3.5.** Everpaper switches off\nQwen3.5's hidden \"thinking\" on LM Studio; in one test a 20-second wait became\nunder a second. A reply that only thinks and never answers now shows a clear\nerror instead of an empty answer."],[59,"**LM Studio or Ollama, if it's running with a chat model.** Everpaper uses\nit as it is and installs nothing for it."],[40,"**LM Studio**, **Ollama**, **Nativ**"],[1147,"**Local AI is now reliable enough to be the primary brain.** Hindsight now asks your local model (Ollama, LM Studio, oMLX) for structured JSON output instead of prompting and hoping — the change that makes a 3–8B local model dependable as your main Second Brain, not a fallback. Cloud stays optional and opt-in."],[438,"**Local AI keeps everything on your Mac.** Setup can prepare a private model\nfor you: Everpaper Native on macOS 27, or a managed oMLX model on earlier\nmacOS. It can also use an LM Studio or Ollama you already run. Your text is\nprocessed locally, with no calls off the Mac."],[1171,"**Local AI now grounds on the real thing.** When you use an on-device provider (Ollama, Apple Foundation Models, or LM Studio), Hindsight no longer strips contact details from what the model sees — names, emails, and phone numbers are preserved so your Second Brain and chat answers reference real people and projects instead of `[REDACTED:name]`. Nothing leaves your Mac, so there's no privacy cost. Secrets (cards, keys, tokens) are still scrubbed for every provider."],[960,"**Local model answers stay concise.** Qwen and LFM2.5 models managed by oMLX\nnow have thinking disabled at the chat-template level, so internal reasoning\ndoes not consume the answer."],[1078,"**Long meetings answer from the right minute.** Recall now quotes the passage that actually matched — minute 40 of an hour-long meeting included — instead of only ever reading a meeting's opening minutes. Natural-language questions also match meetings and files far more reliably, and your indexed folders are searched by meaning, not just exact words."],[1038,"**Long meetings stay light.** MOSS processes audio in ten-minute windows and stitches speakers across them, so memory stays flat (about 5 GB during finalization) no matter how long the meeting runs. The live meeting pane keeps its instant preview; the final pass after you stop is the authoritative transcript."],[348,"**macOS 15 (Sequoia) or later.**"],[381,"**Managed oMLX** on earlier macOS: Everpaper installs oMLX and a model sized\nfor your Mac."],[1128,"**Meaning-aware file chunking.** With a local model configured, files are split into sections by meaning instead of by paragraph or page, so retrieval lands on coherent passages — and it falls back cleanly to structural chunking otherwise. PDFs and spreadsheets keep their page-accurate citations."],[1034,"**Meet MOSS, the new advised meeting model.** Until now, Hindsight transcribed meetings in two passes — one model wrote the words, a second worked out who spoke, and the two were stitched together afterwards. MOSS (MOSS-Transcribe-Diarize, Apache 2.0) does both at once: a single on-device model that transcribes and tells voices apart as it listens, so speaker changes land where sentences change hands. It is advised for meetings and entirely opt-in — about a 2 GB one-time download, and audio never leaves your Mac."],[1110,"**Meeting alerts actually arrive.** Reminders now show as banners while Hindsight is the frontmost app (they were silently suppressed before), a Mac that slept through the reminder window still alerts you on wake while the meeting is joinable — with honest timing (\"starts in 2 minutes\" / \"has started\") — and declining notification permission no longer switches the whole calendar feature off."],[1172,"**Meeting chat speaks up when it fails.** A question to a meeting that can't reach the AI provider now shows the reason inline, instead of going silent or leaving a placeholder message behind."],[474,"**Meeting hotkey**"],[931,"**Meeting status takes less room.** Processing status sits on the meeting's\ndetails line, with Retry visible when something fails. The Transcript,\nOverview, Ask and Speakers tabs are slimmer."],[1202,"**Meeting status updates instantly.** The menu-bar recording glyph, the permission\nprompt when a recording is blocked, and the \"transcribing…\" indicator now reflect\nwhat's happening the moment it changes."],[556,"**Meeting summaries.** Recording and transcription stay on your Mac. If you\nset up AI, the transcript of each recent meeting goes to your AI to write its\n**Summary**. With a cloud provider, this happens only after you turn on\n**Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal**. **Run marked prompts\nautomatically** (Meetings page, off by default) also sends transcripts after\neach meeting."],[541,"**Meeting transcription.** Meetings are recorded and transcribed by\non-device speech models. Recordings are never uploaded."],[1043,"**Meeting transcripts honor the Lexicon mode.** Raw leaves recognized meeting wording untouched; Clean applies your Custom Words corrections during meeting finalization, crash recovery, and Re-analyze."],[884,"**Meetings join the day in order.** Finished meetings enter the account at\nthe time they happened, with more of what was actually said, before the\nevening entry is written."],[875,"**Meetings keep their own paragraphs.** Updates from your screen no longer\nblend into meeting paragraphs; each meeting stays separate and labeled."],[1097,"**Meetings name themselves.** A finished recording takes its title from the overlapping calendar event — no more walls of \"Meeting 23 Jun at 18:37\". Rapid-fire crosstalk renders compactly, phantom \"speakers\" with five words step out of the speaker list, transcript previews read like sentences, prompts show as runnable actions and replace their old results instead of stacking duplicates."],[460,"**Meetings page**: click **Record** on the **Record a meeting** tile."],[1231,"**Meetings wait for the speech model.** If you start recording before the\non-device speech model has finished its one-time setup, Hindsight now shows\n\"Preparing speech model…\" and begins once it's ready, instead of recording a\nmeeting that comes back with an empty transcript."],[686,"**Meetings, by what was heard.** A meeting can be found by its sound, not\nonly its transcript, and the answer can say where in the recording it was\nheard. Its source opens the matching part of the transcript."],[1118,"**Meetings, tightened.** Stereo playback now lines up with the transcript timeline, and the live preview cuts at natural speech pauses instead of mid-word. Plus a batch of fixes: the AI vision-descriptions toggle actually works now, importing from the legacy app no longer freezes the window, and chat vs. embeddings API keys no longer overwrite each other."],[593,"**Meetings** page → After each meeting"],[664,"**Meetings**: a recorded meeting, by title."],[418,"**Meetings**: record and review conversations."],[878,"**Memory chat can read sources directly.** When normal search comes up short,\nAsk reads a limited set of matching sources itself and skips anything no\nlonger visible."],[421,"**Memory** (also called Second Brain): ask questions across everything, and\nsee what Everpaper has learned about your work."],[1270,"**Memory** (the sidebar also calls it **Second Brain**) is where Everpaper\nreflects your work back to you. It reads across every day you've captured,\nyour recorded meetings and the folders you include, links the things that keep\nrecurring, and answers your questions with sources."],[463,"**Menu bar**: click the Everpaper icon and choose **Start Recording**."],[477,"**Microphone + System Audio** (default) records your voice and the call together, as separate time-aligned tracks. **System Audio Only** records just what's playing on your Mac."],[845,"**Microphone changes recover better.** Everpaper copes better when you switch\nto an audio device that uses a different format."],[472,"**Microphone**"],[374,"**Microphone**: for meetings. Press **Allow microphone**."],[545,"**Model downloads**, when you set up speech or AI models (for example with\n**Prepare on this Mac** or **Set up**), or when your first meeting needs the\ntranscription model: from Hugging Face and everpaper.app, and the oMLX server\nfrom GitHub. After setup, the search model also downloads by itself at a\nquiet moment, unless you postponed it or chose &#x2A;*Apple (previous)** under\n**Search model**; see Search by meaning."],[842,"**Model setup checks every file.** Setup verifies the files it downloads and\nprepares both the after-meeting and the live speaker models. Once prepared,\nit works offline."],[1220,"**More accurate speakers.** Tightened speaker separation so a single remote\nparticipant is no longer occasionally split into two, and a slow\nspeaker-detection step no longer holds up the final transcript."],[963,"**MOSS is now the default meeting engine.** First-run setup names the one-time\ndownload before it begins, starts it only when you press the button, and keeps\nworking in the background while setup continues. The standard transcription\npipeline remains available as a fallback until MOSS is ready."],[1203,"**Much better VoiceOver support.** Buttons and controls across the app — Meetings,\nthe Day Journal, the Second Brain, onboarding, and the Index — now announce\nmeaningful names, and secondary text meets WCAG AA contrast."],[794,"**Much lighter while you type.** Adaptive visual history writes a tiny\nfraction of what it used to while you type. In our tests, two minutes of\ntyping went from gigabytes written to under a megabyte, with far less\nprocessor use."],[1214,"**Name a meeting from your calendar.** One click names a meeting after the\ncalendar event it overlaps, so your recordings match your schedule."],[1062,"**Names you can guess.** Sections now lead with plain titles — Meetings, Transcription & Speech, Shortcuts & System, This Copy — with the book's own names kept as quiet subtitles beneath. \"Change my hotkey\" finally has an obvious home."],[52,"**Nativ** is a separate local server app. It is not Everpaper Native. Apple\nIntelligence is not an option in this beta. On macOS 15 and 26, Everpaper\nNative doesn't appear in the list."],[820,"**Native models by name.** In **Settings → AI & summaries**, Everpaper Native\nlists the models on this Mac by name. You can set one up there, or remove one\nyou no longer use."],[388,"**Needs permission**: a permission it needs wasn't granted."],[1226,"**New app icon.** A Daybook cover with the rubric-red ribbon bookmark."],[1209,"**New days appear right away.** After you stop and save a day, it now shows up\non the Days page immediately — previously a freshly finished day could stay\nhidden there until you quit and reopened the app."],[974,"**New intelligence option: Everpaper Native (macOS 27).** On Macs running\nthe macOS 27 beta, the provider picker gains an \"Everpaper Native\" row.\nChoosing it runs summaries, extraction, and chat through an engine bundled\nwith the app and built on Apple's frameworks — no LM Studio, no Ollama, no\nserver to install or keep running. On earlier macOS versions nothing changes\nand the row does not appear."],[841,"**New setups use Nemotron for both.** New installations transcribe and label\nspeakers with Nemotron. Parakeet is still in **Settings → Speech**, and an\nengine you already picked is kept."],[1035,"**New users meet it during setup.** First-run setup gains a \"Tell Speakers Apart\" step: accept to download MOSS in the background while you keep walking through setup, or decline and keep the standard pipeline — nothing blocks, and you can change your mind later in Settings."],[809,"**New: Skip private browsing windows.** A new switch in\n**Settings → Privacy**, on by default. While a private or incognito window is\nin front, Everpaper takes no screenshot and keeps no window title. With the\nbrowser extension it uses the browser's own private-window state; without\nit, it looks for the marker browsers put in the window title."],[808,"**New: Skip sites.** In **Settings → Privacy**, list the websites to keep out\nof screenshots while they're in front. Subdomains are skipped too: skipping\nexample.com also skips mail.example.com. Type an address or tap a\nsuggestion; nothing is skipped until you add it."],[91,"**No AI set up:** nothing is sent."],[437,"**No AI traffic without a provider you choose.** If you don't set one up,\nnothing is sent to any AI service. Recording, transcription, search and your\njournal still work; written summaries and answers wait until you set one up."],[1251,"**No AI traffic without a provider you choose.** Until you connect an AI\nprovider, nothing is sent anywhere — Ask the book and the Second Brain simply\nwait."],[980,"**No functional changes.** This build exists to move Everpaper's release process onto a new machine; everything listed under b78 is included and unchanged. If you are already running b78, there is nothing new to try."],[920,"**No more half-minute wait after a Native answer.** With Everpaper Native, an\nanswer kept running for up to about 35 seconds after its last word appeared,\nholding back its sources and the New conversation button. The engine now\nstops as soon as the answer ends; in testing, that wait dropped to about a\ntenth of a second."],[1228,"**No more waiting to record.** Previously, starting a meeting could pause on\n\"Preparing speech model…\" while the on-device model loaded. Now recording\nbegins immediately and captures audio from the first second; the speech model\nloads in the background."],[844,"**No phantom extra speaker.** A voice that already has a label no longer gets\na second one from a later pass."],[553,"**No provider, no traffic.** If you haven't configured a provider, features\nthat use AI (answers in &#x2A;*Ask your memory…** and Memory's **Ask** tab,\nsummaries) simply wait. Nothing is sent to any AI."],[49,"**None**"],[1240,"**Notarized and stapled.** The app is signed, notarized by Apple, and the\nnotarization ticket is stapled into the build. macOS can verify Hindsight\noffline, so the first launch is clean even without a network connection — no\nspurious \"damaged\" or \"cannot be opened\" warnings."],[489,"**Notes.** Type as the meeting runs; your notes save as you go. The flag button\n(for example **Add 3:42**, showing the current time) stamps the recording time\ninto your notes."],[902,"**Nothing is cut mid-field on the way to the AI.** Each piece of evidence is\nsent whole or not at all, sized to what your model can read."],[1017,"**Nothing is ever locked away.** An expired beta is read-only, not closed: your meetings, your days, search, and export all keep working exactly as before. The recordings on your Mac are yours, and no expiry touches them."],[129,"**Notify before meetings**: a quiet note slips in before each meeting begins.\nNothing records on its own."],[1310,"**Off**: no automatic checks."],[128,"**Off**: no reminders and no countdowns. This is the default."],[93,"**oMLX, LM Studio, Ollama, Nativ:** private while their address is on this\nMac, which is the default. Point one at another computer and Everpaper treats\nit like a cloud provider."],[37,"**oMLX**"],[554,"**On-device providers keep everything on your Mac**: Everpaper Native, or\nOllama, LM Studio, oMLX or Nativ while their address is on this Mac, which is\nthe default. If you point one of them at another computer, Everpaper treats it\nlike a cloud provider. For an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, you decide with\n**This is a trusted, private endpoint**. Trusted endpoints receive text\nwithout the cloud switch, with only secrets removed, so turn it on only for a\nserver you control."],[270,"**On-screen text.** Each screenshot is read on this Mac, so its text is\nsearchable."],[604,"**on**: a voice is stored only after you name it; &#x2A;*Manage…** forgets one; switching off deletes them all"],[883,"**One account that grows through the day.** The journal no longer writes\nseparate summaries that each lose what came before. It carries a single\nrunning account and adds to it at each update."],[627,"**One day**: on its page in **Timeline**, **Delete this day and all its\nevidence**. You can undo it from Today right after."],[1063,"**One grammar for every control.** Actions that sit beside a status word are real buttons now (Test Input, Test Connection, Download), and Advanced settings live in a visibly inset group at the end of their section — no more guessing what's inside."],[903,"**One long meeting no longer fills the entry.** Meeting excerpts are kept to a\nfair share and placed in time, so the rest of the day still appears."],[628,"**One meeting**: in **Meetings**, right-click it and choose **Delete**. Its\naudio goes too, and this can't be undone."],[937,"**One opt-in now covers what you do and what you type.** Turn on Computer\nHistory once and it records clicks, shortcuts, short selections and\nencrypted typed text in ordinary apps and sites, unless you exclude them.\nIf you already use it, nothing widens until you choose. Password fields and\nprivate browsing are never recorded."],[894,"**One selection style.** The highlight in both sidebars glides to what you\npick; with Reduce Motion it moves at once."],[1173,"**One Send, everywhere.** The ask boxes in the meeting panel, meeting detail, and journal chats now share the same look and the same Send control — a consistent composer across the whole app."],[823,"**One-time setup cards are back.** Recent betas carried a name suffix that\nsilently held back Everpaper's one-time setup cards. This build doesn't, so\nyou may now see the speaker-labels card b97 meant to show."],[94,"**OpenAI-Compatible:** private only while **This is a trusted, private\nendpoint** is on. It starts on for an address on this Mac and off for any\nother. Turn it on only for a server you control: a trusted endpoint receives\ntext without the cloud switch, with only secrets removed."],[43,"**OpenAI-Compatible**"],[995,"**Opening Settings no longer freezes the app.** Since b72, opening Settings could pin the processor and beachball the whole app for ten seconds or more — on some Macs indefinitely. A bookkeeping write fired on every screen refresh while the page was open, and each write forced another refresh. The page now reads that record without touching it, and the loop is gone."],[913,"**Optional precise word timing.** On Macs with 24 GB or more, an optional\n1.8 GB model refines when each word starts and ends, after transcription. It\ndownloads only when you press Prepare, adds some processing time, and never\nchanges the words or who said them."],[574,"**Other people.** Communication apps are included unless you exclude or pause\nthem. Capture other people's communications only with their prior consent."],[62,"**Otherwise, Managed oMLX**: Gemma 4 E4B on Macs with 24 GB or more,\nLFM2.5 2.6B below that. On macOS 27 Macs under 16 GB, Everpaper Native with\nQwen3 4B instead."],[282,"**Pause when you’re away** stops screenshots when you step away. It is on by\ndefault, after **2 minutes** (or 30 seconds, 1 minute), and appears in Screen\nhistory."],[313,"**People/**: one note per person, built from the email addresses in your\nhistory. Addresses are humanized into names (`jane.smith@example.com` becomes\n*Jane Smith*), and misread copies of the same address collapse into a single\nnote. Each note lists the days that person showed up."],[572,"**Permissions.** **Accessibility**, to read the controls and text you chose,\nand **Input Monitoring**, to notice clicks and shortcuts. Grant them in setup\nor in **Settings → Daily history → Set up permissions**."],[677,"**Phone numbers**"],[1139,"**Pick your embeddings model.** Folder Context RAG now has its own provider config in Settings → Intelligence → File Index, parallel to your chat AI. Point it at Harrier OSS v1 0.6B (or any embedding model) in your Ollama, LM Studio, or oMLX instance — independent of your chat model. A refresh-from-provider list helps you pick. With none set, files use the on-device model."],[482,"**Playback mix**"],[63,"**Prepare on this Mac** also downloads the search model, and, if you chose\nmeetings, the Everyday meeting models for live captions and speaker labels.\n**Set up later** postpones the search model by a week. The Detailed meeting\nmodel isn't part of setup; add it later in **Settings → Speech** (see\nEveryday or Detailed)."],[386,"**Preparing**: still downloading or getting ready in the background."],[1086,"**Press ⌥Space anywhere.** A floating slip of the book appears over whatever you're doing — another app, another Space, even a full-screen call — without stealing focus from your work. Type to search your days, meetings, beliefs, and settings instantly; arrow keys and Return jump straight to the page in Hindsight."],[431,"**Private AI on your Mac**: Everpaper Native on macOS 27 (Gemma 4 E4B with\n16 GB of memory or more), or a managed local model on earlier macOS. See\nAI on your Mac."],[1200,"**Private by default.** All of this stays on your Mac and works with on-device AI. Sharing\nyour profile with a cloud provider takes a separate, explicit opt-in."],[379,"**Private summaries, on your Mac.** A card headed **Recommended for this\nMac** shows one setup, with a status line each for **Meetings** (if you chose\nthem), **Summaries** and **Search**. If you already have an AI setup, it's\nkept. If LM Studio or Ollama is already running with a chat model, it's used\nas it is. Otherwise:"],[1066,"**Prose is serif everywhere now.** The sidebar, Today's Focus, the Ask bar, chat copy, previews, empty states, and settings descriptions all read in the book's serif — the near-monospace face is retired to what it does best: timers, folios, stamps, and captured text."],[940,"**Quiet stretches stay short.** A long run of switching between windows no\nlonger gets written up as if it were real work."],[1012,"**Quieter, more careful internals.** File names no longer land in the system log where a diagnostics bundle could carry them; the command-line tools run stored transcripts through the same credential-stripping pass as the app; deleting a day keeps its screenshots restorable for the full undo window; and several heavy list and audio operations got lighter."],[1010,"**Re-analysis shows its percentage on every engine.** The progress bar was still wired to the old assumption that only one engine could report."],[1212,"**Re-analyze a meeting.** A new button on a saved meeting re-runs transcription\nand speaker detection on the original audio — so you can pick up these speaker\nimprovements on meetings recorded earlier, without recording again."],[1044,"**Re-analyze stays consistent.** When you rerun transcription and speaker separation on a completed meeting, Hindsight uses the current Raw/Clean setting instead of silently applying corrections every time."],[834,"**Ready before your first meeting.** Today warns you if transcription isn't\nset up, with one Set up button and the download size. If you skip it, the\nfirst meeting downloads the model with progress, and the audio is always\nrecorded. A missing model no longer stops a meeting, discarded recordings\nstay discarded, a microphone that goes silent is restarted, and merging\nspeakers keeps the right names."],[385,"**Ready**"],[991,"**Reasoning models stop thinking out loud.** Models that emit their scratch work before the answer no longer leak it into summaries and notes, whichever local server you use."],[1206,"**Rebindable meeting hotkey.** The global start/stop shortcut (default ⌘⇧M) can now\nbe changed in Settings → The Minutes."],[932,"**Recall connects a promise to what followed.** Asking the Concordance about a\ncommitment now cites both the meeting where it was made and the later work on\nit."],[510,"**recognised from an earlier meeting**: a voice you named before."],[858,"**Record now during a workday is steadier.** While your workday is being\nrecorded, pressing **Record now** switches Today straight to a \"workday\npaused\" status, instead of keeping the live control running through the\nchange. This targets a crash seen on b93."],[1178,"**Recoverable deletions.** Deleting a day now removes it from the book but offers an *Undo* right after, from Today — so a mistake never costs you a captured day."],[283,"**Reduce work on battery** lightens capture and analysis on battery. Off by\ndefault."],[1215,"**Refreshed meeting tabs.** The Transcript / Results / Chat / Speakers tabs now\nuse the same thumb-tab style as the rest of the app."],[793,"**Related moments.** When a meeting starts, or a document stays in front for\na little while, Everpaper can quietly list up to three related past moments,\nmeetings or files: on the meeting desk for meetings, on Today for documents.\nThere are no notifications, and skipped apps, skipped sites and private\nwindows never trigger it. Turn it off with **Show related moments** in\n**Settings → Speech**."],[491,"**Related.** When a meeting starts, a quiet line under your notes can show\n**1 related** or **3 related**: past moments, meetings or files that match\nthis one. Only the count shows, since the panel is often shared on screen;\nclick it to see the titles, or ✕ to hide them for this meeting. Turn it off\nwith **Show related moments** in **Settings → Speech**."],[397,"**Relaunch Everpaper.** macOS only applies this permission when the app\nstarts, so the status may not change until you quit and reopen. Setup shows\na **Relaunch Everpaper** button when it notices the grant hasn't taken\neffect yet."],[603,"**Remember voices between meetings**"],[1130,"**Reminders that behave.** \"Add to Reminders\" confirms when it worked, won't pile up duplicates, and writes the whole list in one pass — and an imminent meeting's reminder is no longer dropped when notification permission was still warming up."],[1213,"**Rename a meeting.** A pencil next to the title renames a meeting at any time."],[1117,"**Retention that means it.** A new toggle — \"Also delete their text & recall data\" — makes the retention setting remove expired captures' text, search index, and recall entities too, so old data truly expires and the database stays bounded. It's on for new installs; existing journals keep their history unless you opt in. Cleanup now also runs daily, not just when a session starts."],[1142,"**Retrieval uses the right model.** A matching fix ensures the query vectors and the indexed vectors come from the same model, so semantic search actually works (it was silently mismatched before)."],[1180,"**Richer timeline.** Every activity row now shows a thumbnail you can click to open full-size — not just the starred moments."],[498,"**Right-click** for **Stop**, **Pause** / **Resume**, **Open** and\n&#x2A;*Cancel…** (Cancel asks before deleting the recording). After the meeting is\nsaved, right-click offers **Open Meeting** and **Dismiss**."],[591,"**Run marked prompts automatically**"],[822,"**Running notes are protected.** If a rewrite of the day's running notes\ndrops more than half of them, Everpaper keeps the earlier notes and adds the\nnew ones after."],[1140,"**Saves take effect immediately.** Configuring the embeddings model used to require an app relaunch (a stale-capture bug froze the old config). Now the index and retrieval read the live setting on every call — change the model, hit re-index, and it's used right away."],[1088,"**Scopes and files.** ⌘1–4 narrows to everything, days, meetings, or files. File matches search your indexed folders and reveal the document in Finder."],[373,"**Screen and system audio**: for meetings (to hear the other side of a call)\nand for Screen history or Detailed screen history. Press **Allow access**.\nOnce it's granted, macOS also asks separately for **System Audio\nRecording**, a lighter way for meetings to hear the call."],[540,"**Screen capture and text recognition.** In the screen history modes,\nEverpaper takes screenshots of your displays and reads them on this Mac.\nScreenshots are stored locally and never uploaded."],[256,"**Screen history** (Settings: Visual history), the default"],[367,"**Screen history** (the default): full-display screenshots at your chosen\ninterval, read on this Mac so you can search what you saw. They use more\nstorage and can include windows behind the one you're using."],[791,"**Screenshots and meeting audio, searchable.** On macOS 27, a screenshot can\nbe found by describing what it shows, even with no readable text, and a\nmeeting recording by its sound, not only its transcript; the result opens\nthe matching part of the transcript. Before macOS 27, search by meaning works\nfor text."],[685,"**Screenshots, by what they show.** Describe a picture, a chart or a design,\nand Everpaper can find the screenshot even when it has no readable text."],[268,"**Screenshots** (Screen history and Detailed screen history). An image of\neach whole display at the interval you choose: **every 2 minutes** by\ndefault, or 30 seconds, 1, 5 or 10 minutes (**Screenshot every**, shown in\nScreen history). It can\ninclude windows behind the one you're using."],[433,"**Search by meaning**: an on-device search model finds things described in\nother words or another language; on macOS 27, also screenshots by what they\nshow and meetings by what was heard. See\nSearch by meaning."],[621,"**Search model**"],[789,"**Search that understands what you mean.** A new on-device search model,\nEmbeddingGemma-2, searches your memory, files and meetings. It finds the\nright moment even when you describe it in other words, or in another\nlanguage, and exact names and phrases still match first. Your searches stay\non this Mac."],[1182,"**Secret-bearing frames never leave your Mac.** If a screenshot's captured text revealed a card, key, or token, its pixels are no longer sent to a vision provider — the text redactor can't see into the image, so the whole frame is withheld."],[1123,"**See — and pause — file indexing.** Indexing a folder now shows a real progress bar (files done out of the total) instead of an open-ended spinner, and you can pause a long index and resume it later from where it left off. Pausing never leaves a half-built index behind."],[1151,"**Send to Reminders, not Notes.** The export now creates one checkable reminder per todo in a \"Hindsight\" list — native checklist, individually completable, syncs to your iPhone. Action items belong in Reminders."],[387,"**Set up later**"],[383,"**Set up later** skips all of it: recording and your journal still work, your\nfirst meeting downloads the transcription model, and the search model waits a\nweek before it sets itself up in the background. **Choose advanced options**\nlists every route, including **Use my own provider** (a cloud service or your\nown endpoint) and **No AI for now**; **Use recommended setup** takes you back\nto the card. Press **Review my setup** to continue.\nAI on your Mac explains each option."],[792,"**Setting up search.** The search model is a one-time download of about\n1.5 GB. New users can prepare it during setup; existing users see a card\noffering it. It prepares in the background at a quiet moment, search works\nas before until it's ready, and older history is brought up to date\ngradually. If you cancel or choose Set up later, Everpaper waits a week\nbefore downloading it in the background. To go back, choose Apple (previous)\nunder **Search model** in **Settings → AI & summaries**; nothing is lost."],[87,"**Settings → AI & summaries → AI connection → llama.cpp engine\n(experimental)** is off by default. Turned on, it runs Gemma 4 E4B on this Mac\nthrough a different engine, which is lighter on the Mac while it writes but\nslower to read long prompts. It needs a Mac with at least 16 GB of memory and\ndownloads its model (about 5.2 GB) the first time. Everpaper switches only\nafter the model has answered a first request. While it's on, **AI option**\nreads **llama.cpp engine**. Turn the switch off to go back to the AI option\nyou had before."],[90,"**Settings → General → AI work** shows whether **Background AI** is running\nor paused and why (for example **Paused: microphone in use by** and the app's\nname); Today shows the same line while work is paused. **AI use today** lists\nhow much each feature used the AI today."],[1315,"**Settings → General → Updates → Check for Updates…**"],[893,"**Settings opens from the sidebar.** Settings slides in from the main\nwindow's sidebar, and Back to Everpaper sits in its own sidebar."],[892,"**Settings pages with plain names.** Capture is now Daily history,\nTranscription & Speech is Speech, Intelligence is AI & summaries, Privacy &\nData is Privacy, Shortcuts & System is General, and This Copy is About.\nControls are standard Mac switches, menus and buttons, grouped into sections,\nand Privacy is simpler to move around."],[1100,"**Settings you can navigate.** The section index stays pinned while you scroll, ⌘F hint included; destructive actions look like the buttons they are; and the Lexicon finally dresses like the rest of the book."],[1167,"**Settings you can trust.** Three toggles that promised behavior but never actually did anything have been fixed or removed. The cloud-consent controls are now one clear decision instead of three cross-referencing toggles."],[667,"**Settings**: any setting, landing on its own row."],[422,"**Settings**."],[788,"**Setup chooses nothing for you.** Moving through the pages changes nothing:\nsettings are saved only when you pick something, and models download only\nwhen you press Prepare. Permission buttons ask macOS directly and offer a\nshortcut to System Settings if a request isn't granted. You finish on Today\nor Meetings, depending on what you chose, and finishing never starts a\nrecording. Run setup again from **Settings → General**. The animated 3D\nribbon and the \"Make yourself at home\" banner are gone."],[1055,"**Setup finally teaches the good keys.** The rehearsal now covers ⌥Space — your memory from anywhere on the Mac — alongside ⌘K, and the meetings step mentions ⌘⇧M for starting a recording from any app."],[957,"**Setup is three screens instead of eleven.** Choose what Everpaper should\nremember — your meetings, your days, or both — and setup asks only for what\nthat choice needs."],[927,"**Setup keeps your place.** Quit halfway and setup reopens where you were;\ngoing back keeps your choices. It explains permissions, downloads and private\nprocessing before you act. You can run it again from Settings, and finishing\nopens Today or Meetings without starting a recording."],[818,"**Setup picks it for you.** On these Macs, guided setup now chooses\nEverpaper Native with Gemma. A running LM Studio or Ollama with a chat model\nis still used, and an AI setup you already have is kept. macOS 27 Macs with\nless than 16 GB keep Qwen3 4B; earlier macOS versions work as in b97."],[588,"**Share your profile with cloud AI**"],[1195,"**Sharper recall.** Asking about a specific person or file now surfaces the right ones\nto jump to, instead of coming up empty on a normally-worded question."],[843,"**Short live snippets keep their last word.** A word at the edge of a short\nlive chunk is no longer dropped."],[618,"**Show related moments**"],[941,"**Simpler AI choice in setup.** \"Choose for me\" picks Everpaper Native with\nMOSS on macOS 27 and a managed local engine with MOSS on earlier systems.\n\"I manage local AI\" keeps every option visible. Nothing downloads until you\npress Prepare."],[1122,"**Single-microphone recordings get real speakers.** A meeting captured from a single audio source — just your microphone, or one where the system audio dropped partway through — used to collapse into one speaker labelled \"Me\". Now Hindsight splits it into separate speakers whenever it hears more than one voice, while a genuine solo recording still reads as \"Me\". Re-analyzing an existing meeting picks this up too."],[615,"**Skip apps**, **Skip sites**"],[577,"**Skip apps**: apps Everpaper never captures while they are in front. No\napps are skipped until you add them."],[612,"**Skip private browsing windows**"],[579,"**Skip private browsing windows**, on by default: while a private or\nincognito window is in front, Everpaper takes no screenshot and keeps no\nwindow title. With the Everpaper browser extension it uses the browser's\nown private-window state; without it, it looks for the marker browsers put\nin the window title. Detection is best effort, and a private window it\ncan't recognize is treated as a normal one, so add sensitive sites to\n**Skip sites** as well."],[578,"**Skip sites**: websites to keep out of screenshots while they're in front.\nSubdomains count too: skipping example.com also skips mail.example.com.\nType an address, or tap a suggestion (common email, chat and messaging\nsites). Nothing is skipped until you add it. Email and chat aren't left out\non their own, so add them here if you want them kept out."],[1073,"**Small consistency fixes.** The Library page now matches its sidebar name (\"The Archive\"), meeting dates share one format everywhere, date group headers match the app's eyebrow style, the engine status chip no longer fuses with the Import Transcript… button, and a meeting that lives on two calendars shows once in Upcoming instead of twice."],[1207,"**Smaller fixes.** Onboarding scrolls on small displays, the meeting audio player\nresets at the end of a clip, deleting a custom word asks first, and a failed app\nsummary can be retried."],[1004,"**Smaller honesty fixes throughout.** The menu bar says \"Transcribing…\" instead of a disabled \"Stop Recording\"; an in-flight transcription no longer disappears from the list while you type in search; a long error message fits inside the pill instead of being cut off; and a rare stall in speaker separation can no longer hold a finished transcript hostage."],[1127,"**Smarter suggested to-dos.** Today's task suggestions are rephrased into natural, imperative tasks; meeting action items are extracted by the model from the meeting text; and tasks you pull from a specific meeting now stay on the rail when the page refreshes. Each step falls back to the built-in, on-device behavior when no model is connected."],[887,"**Smoother Memory and sidebar.** Switching sections stutters less."],[1238,"**Smoother Screen Recording grant.** After you allow Screen Recording, setup\nnow shows a clear **Relaunch Hindsight** step — macOS only applies this\npermission to a freshly launched app, so the relaunch is what makes the grant\ntake effect."],[869,"**Smoother sidebar and Settings.** The selection highlight uses one animation\ninstead of two, and Settings no longer rebuilds itself on every section\nchange."],[1116,"**Snappier everywhere.** Sending a question paints instantly instead of waiting on retrieval, the Second Brain tab opens without a hitch, and recall, day chats, and the Days list no longer block the interface while they read the journal."],[1204,"**Snappier reading.** Opening a finished day, searching the Index, and reading\nstreamed answers are all faster and smoother."],[921,"**Sources arrive with the answer.** A Memory answer shows its full source\nlist as soon as it finishes, without switching tabs."],[1067,"**Start means start.** The day journal's Start button is now sized like the page's main action instead of a small chip under the feature list."],[130,"**Start recording automatically**: Everpaper gives you five seconds to\ncancel, then starts recording."],[1241,"**Steadier installs.** Hardened the DMG so dragging Hindsight to Applications\nand opening it the first time just works."],[483,"**Stereo** (default; your voice and the call on separate channels) or **Mono** (one blended channel, smaller)."],[852,"**Stopping the workday twice is safe.** Stop requests are handled one at a\ntime."],[1235,"**Sturdier packaging.** The disk image is now signed unambiguously, and the\ndiagnostics behind the Screen Recording grant were tightened so first-run\nsetup issues are easier to trace."],[1108,"**Suggested to-dos you can trust.** AI-extracted tasks are now checked against the meeting or screen text they claim to come from — an invented task never reaches the rail or your Reminders. Rephrased titles that drift from the original task fall back to the plain version, owner-style items (\"Jane: send the brief\") are picked up, and filler lines (\"None\", \"No action items identified\") no longer become to-dos."],[1095,"**Suggestions you can trust.** Tell Hindsight your email addresses (Settings → Second Brain) and it stops mistaking your own name for a person to track — no more \"assess your own relevance\" suggestions. Speculative observations can't become Apple Reminders anymore, websites and people no longer masquerade as tasks, and the same insight stops appearing twice in different words."],[1037,"**Switching back is one click.** \"Use instead\" returns meetings to the standard Parakeet pipeline at any time; the downloaded model is never deleted, and typing, dictation, and file transcription keep using Parakeet or Whisper exactly as before."],[484,"**System audio route**"],[895,"**System lettering for labels.** Navigation, Today's labels and shared\ncontrols use the Mac's system font. Journals, transcripts and answers keep\ntheir reading typeface."],[1135,"**Test Connection for embeddings.** The embeddings provider card now has a \"Test Connection\" button (just like the chat AI). It sends a tiny embed request to your configured endpoint and tells you whether it worked — including the served model and vector dimensions — or shows the exact error. No more guessing whether your model is loaded or the name is right."],[905,"**Text-only models don't get screenshots.** With LM Studio, Everpaper checks\nwhether the selected model can read images before sending any."],[432,"**The Concordance**: press ⌥Space in any app to find or ask about\na day, a meeting or a file."],[1177,"**The Day Index.** Press `⌘L` anywhere to open a chronological overview of every captured day — title, date, and a thumbnail for each, with a density strip showing how much history you've built. Use `↑↓` to move and `Return` to turn straight to a day."],[1029,"**The day journal stops saying it is recording when it is not.** Screen capture pauses while a meeting records; the journal now says so, instead of showing \"Recording\" above a frame count that cannot move. Marking a moment still works throughout."],[663,"**The Days**: any finished day, by title, summary, or date."],[1054,"**The download no longer holds you hostage.** The speech model starts downloading the moment setup opens and keeps going while you walk the earlier steps — by the time you reach the models page it's often already done."],[914,"**The end of a recording is never skipped.** Audio reading continues through\nshort reads, so the last part of a recording can't be silently left out."],[1009,"**The expired-beta message actually appears everywhere.** In b72, an expired build refused to start the day journal silently — the Start button just did nothing. It now explains itself there too, and Settings keeps saying a build has expired instead of going quiet at that exact moment. (This build does not expire.)"],[904,"**The journal says only what its sources show.** A rewrite can't turn a\nrequest into a finished task, or a scheduling question into an email you\nreceived. Where a section can't be supported, Everpaper shows the source\npassage instead."],[1045,"**The Lexicon is clearer.** The Processing mode copy now says it applies to both file transcriptions and meeting transcripts, and Custom Words are described as Clean-mode corrections."],[1011,"**The menu bar stops claiming \"Capturing\" during a meeting.** It now says \"Paused for meeting\", matching what the app actually does."],[911,"**The MOSS meeting model runs leaner.** This beta uses a lighter build of the\nsame model and no longer decodes a short first attempt twice. Earlier tests\non two recordings showed about a fifth less processing time and about 30%\nless peak memory; one longer recording came out slightly worse. The original\nmodel remains the fallback, and a change takes effect after relaunch."],[1015,"**The other two transcription engines report their progress now.** Until this build only the advised meeting model showed a percentage; the standard pipeline — the one most people are on — spun with nothing to say for the whole wait. Both now count up, in the pill and in the meetings list. They report a plain percentage rather than naming the part of the recording being worked on, so it is coarser than the advised model, but it moves."],[1145,"**The page now leads with what the AI actually did.** The Second Brain used to open with templated counting (\"X sits at the center of a lot of what you do\", cycling a few phrases). Now the AI Reflection — grounded observations written from your real captured moments — leads the page, and the templated belief cards are rephrased into varied, specific observations by the model. The vanity stat row is gone. With no AI connected, you get an honest \"connect a model\" prompt instead of fake templated confidence."],[1002,"**The percentage no longer vanishes mid-way.** On a two-track recording the number used to disappear for minutes at the halfway mark; it now carries through."],[1024,"**The pill counts up.** After you stop a meeting, the floating pill now reads \"Transcribing… 47%\" instead of spinning with nothing to say. The percentage comes from the advised meeting model; the standard pipeline still shows a plain spinner."],[999,"**The pill is clickable now — in every state.** While a transcription runs, clicking the floating pill takes you straight to the Meetings page, where the working row shows the step, the percentage, and the time estimate. When it says \"Saved\", clicking opens the finished meeting. It used to swallow the click silently; right-click and VoiceOver now offer the same routes."],[1060,"**The Press Check.** Settings now opens with its own health: anything unhealthy — microphone, screen recording, a failed AI test, a missing speech model — appears as one line with its one fix. When all is well, it says so in a single quiet line and stays out of the way."],[985,"**The recommendation fits your hardware.** Everpaper reads this Mac's memory and only offers models it can actually hold alongside transcription. LFM2.5 2.6B stays the default everywhere; a faster lightweight option is always available, and Macs with 24 GB or more can pick a larger Qwen model for higher-quality summaries."],[1148,"**The Reflection checks itself, and sees the right frames.** Every reflection is now verified against your actual captured data before it sticks — a fabricated name paired with a real citation gets stripped. And it retrieves semantically-relevant frames (not just the most recent ones), so a reflection about \"the migration\" pulls migration moments from across the week."],[1160,"**The Reflection.** After you finish a day (or when you open Hindsight in the morning), your Second Brain reads the actual moments it captured — not just the counts, but what was on your screen, in what order, at what time — and writes a short Reflection: two or three genuine observations a thoughtful colleague might make. Every claim points back to the specific moments it noticed it in. It runs on your Mac if you use Apple Foundation Models or Ollama — free, private, no cloud needed."],[1162,"**The story of becoming.** The \"Who you are\" card on the You tab now tells a narrative — what the Second Brain has come to understand about you, how that's changed recently, and where it sees tension."],[488,"**The top row** shows **RECORDING** or **PAUSED**, the elapsed time and a live\nwaveform (it reads **ROOM QUIET** when nobody is talking). On the right: **Mute\nmic** (Microphone + System Audio only), **Pause** / **Resume**, and **Stop\nrecording**. Pausing leaves no gap: the saved audio plays back seamlessly."],[1016,"**This beta stops recording after fifteen days.** A beta never updates itself, so an old copy would otherwise drift on forever, unsupported. When the date passes, Everpaper declines to start a *new* recording and points you at the current build."],[1188,"**Tighter privacy at the edge.** When a cloud model is in use, email addresses and phone\nnumbers are scrubbed from what's sent — on top of the existing secret redaction."],[1191,"**Timeline previews.** Each moment in a day's timeline now shows a thumbnail you can click\nto open full-size."],[419,"**Timeline**: your finished days, one per page."],[1163,"**Today's Focus.** A new panel on Today surfaces suggested to-dos from your patterns — files you opened then dropped, topics that faded, action items from meetings. Check one off, dismiss it, or send the whole list to Apple Notes with one click."],[288,"**Today's focus** on the Today page suggests a few to-dos. Without AI it finds\n**open loops** (files or topics you touched, then dropped) and **cooling\nthreads** (things that went quiet). With AI set up, it also pulls to-dos from\nyour recent meetings and days, and turns the action items in your meeting\nresults into tasks. These appear first."],[461,"**Today**: click **Record now** on the **Next meeting** card. The recording\nis named after your next calendar event if there is one."],[417,"**Today**: your day so far, the journal control, your next meeting and\nToday's focus."],[1155,"**Todos from real content, not just patterns.** When an AI provider is connected, Today's Focus now reads the actual text of your meeting transcripts and on-screen captures — not just the statistics — and extracts specific, actionable tasks (\"Send the brief to Jane by Friday\") instead of pattern inferences (\"You haven't touched plan.md in 8 days\")."],[396,"**Toggle Everpaper on.** On recent macOS there's no confirmation dialog —\nflipping the toggle is the grant."],[316,"**Topics/**: \"area\" notes for the clusters Everpaper detected in your graph,\neach linking its members."],[1079,"**Transcript import, hardened.** Teams and Windows exports (CRLF line endings) import correctly, Zoom-style \"Name:\" speakers are recognized, timings survive so exporting an imported meeting as srt/vtt works, and big imports no longer freeze the app — the imported meeting opens for you when it's done."],[1219,"**Transcription no longer freezes.** A scheduling deadlock could leave the live\ntranscript blank during a meeting and the post-recording \"transcribing…\" step\nstuck indefinitely. Recording and transcription now run on separate lanes — the\nlive transcript fills in during the meeting and the final transcript completes\npromptly."],[1071,"**Transcripts read like a page, not a terminal.** Meeting transcripts are now set in the book's serif at a comfortable reading width with proper line spacing — an hour-long conversation no longer renders as full-width monospace. Timestamps and speaker chips stay in the data register."],[546,"**Update checks** to everpaper.app, daily by default and never while a\nmeeting is recording. Choose how they work, or turn them off, in **Settings →\nGeneral → Updates**."],[965,"**Very short recordings are handled honestly.** Recordings under five seconds\nare discarded instead of producing empty meetings, with a clear explanation\nin the recording pill."],[1222,"**Visible page controls.** The Days page now has previous / next buttons and a\ndotted day indicator beneath it — one dot per day, with the current day\nhighlighted. Click any dot to jump straight to that day. (The page-turn\ncontrols were previously invisible edge zones.)"],[1229,"**Visible transcription progress.** While the model loads, the transcript area\nshows a progress bar instead of a frozen screen, and the transcript fills in as\nsoon as the model is ready. The full meeting is always transcribed when you\nstop."],[799,"**Visual history keeps going while you read, watch or meet.** Adaptive\nvisual history used to pause when you weren't typing or clicking. Now it\nkeeps its rhythm, at most one checkpoint every 30 seconds and only when the\nscreen actually changed, including during recorded meetings, with your skip\nrules applied. Nothing is captured while the display sleeps or the screen is\nlocked."],[630,"**Voices**: forget one in **Remembered voices** (&#x2A;*Manage…**), or all of them\nby turning off **Remember voices between meetings**."],[570,"**What it never records.** Password fields and private browsing."],[569,"**What it records.** Clicks, shortcuts, text you select (**Text you select**)\nand text you type (**Text you type**), in all apps and websites unless you\nexclude them. This can include email and messages. You can turn selected or\ntyped text off on its own."],[363,"**What would you like to remember?** Choose **My meetings**, **My days** or\n**Both** (or press 1, 2 or 3). You can add the rest later in Settings."],[575,"**What your AI sees.** While it's kept, the text you select and type is part of\nwhat your AI reads to write the day's running notes and entry, and those can\nquote or summarize it. They stay after the 48 hours, under\nHow long it's kept; delete the day to remove them. It is\nstored only on this Mac. A cloud AI receives it only after you turn on **Allow\ncloud AI to analyze your journal**; a trusted endpoint receives it without that\nswitch."],[269,"**What's left out.** Nothing is captured while what's in front is an app\nunder **Skip apps**, a site under **Skip sites**, or a private browsing\nwindow (**Skip private browsing windows**, on by default), all in\n**Settings → Privacy**. The timed screenshots and Detailed screen history's\nextra images follow the same rules. Nothing is captured while the display\nis asleep, and Detailed screen history's extra images also stop while the\nscreen is locked. See\nKeeping apps and sites out."],[133,"**When it begins**"],[810,"**Window shots in browsers, with or without the extension.** Adaptive visual\nhistory now takes window shots in browsers without the Everpaper extension\ntoo, reading the page address and window title through macOS Accessibility.\nSkipped sites and private windows are still left out."],[1284,"**Worth confronting**: up to five frank observations, each shown with its\nevidence. They appear at the default tone, **Balanced**, and at **Candid**. To\nhide them, choose **Supportive** in **Settings → AI & summaries → Your memory\n& summaries → Second Brain tone**. If your history shows nothing worth\nconfronting, the section stays empty."],[1283,"**Worth noticing**: patterns rising or cooling across your recent days. Each\ncan become a draft: &#x2A;*Draft email…** opens a preview you send with **Open in\nMail**, and &#x2A;*Draft follow-up event…** one you save with **Add to Calendar**.\nNothing is sent or saved until you choose. Drafts don't need AI."],[1018,"**You get warned first.** Three days out, Settings opens with a line saying the build is about to stop recording, and a button that takes you to the current one."],[547,"**Your AI provider**, and an **External embeddings provider** if you set\none, under the rules below. Search by meaning runs on this Mac and sends\nnothing."],[384,"**Your Everpaper, ready when you are.** A receipt of what you chose, one line\neach. For your days: **Your workday**, plus **Clicks and text** with Detailed\nscreen history or Activity only. For meetings: **Your conversations** and\n**Meeting transcripts**. Then **Summaries and questions** and **Your\nprivacy**. Each line except Your privacy says honestly where it stands:"],[542,"**Your journal and graph.** The journal database, the people, files, sites,\nand beliefs in Memory (also called Second Brain), and your meeting library\nare all stored on this Mac."],[61,"**Your local app, once it's ready.** If LM Studio is installed but not\nrunning, or LM Studio or Ollama is running without a chat model, setup says\nso. Start it or load a model, then press **Rescan**."],[1286,"**Your network** lists **Most connected** (the hubs, with how many connections\neach has), **Surprising connections** (pairs seen together more often than\nchance would explain) and **Areas** (clusters). **Show graph** draws up to 120\nrecurring things and their links. When Ask is available, suggested questions\nunder **Ask** open the Ask tab and ask them."],[1146,"**Your Second Brain can now read your files.** Point Hindsight at a workspace folder — a repo, a docs folder, a papers folder — and it indexes everything inside on your Mac: code, markdown, PDFs, images (via OCR), spreadsheets, rich text, email. The Reflection can then connect your screen activity to your own work artifacts, cited by file. \"You switched to Slack right after the compile errors your plan flagged.\" Nothing leaves your Mac; embeddings run on your local model."],[1050,"**Your server, your choice.** Prefer LM Studio? One click opens its download page and Hindsight connects automatically as soon as its server appears. Already running Ollama, LM Studio, or oMLX? Setup finds it and connects with one click, exactly as before. And on Macs with Apple Intelligence available, there's a zero-download Connect that uses the on-device Apple model."],[1089,"**Your shortcut, your call.** Rebind the summon in Settings → The Apparatus. (We default to ⌥Space rather than a global ⌘K on purpose — a system-wide ⌘K would silently eat the command palette in your editor, browser, and Slack. Inside Hindsight, ⌘K remains the Index.)"],[201,"`--allow-insecure-http`: permits plain `http` to a remote cloud-style\nendpoint (your key travels unencrypted)."],[200,"`--base-url`: an absolute `http(s)` URL for self-hosted endpoints."],[187,"`--diarize`: separate speakers and label each turn (Speaker 1, Speaker 2, …).\nWith `nemotron` it uses Nemotron's speaker labels, and with `moss` the\nmodel's own. The acoustic diarizer (used by the other engines, and as\nNemotron's fallback) is tuned with `--diar-max-distance` (default `0.775`;\nhigher merges more voices into fewer speakers), `--diar-merge-similarity`\n(default `0.85`), `--diar-min-speaker-ms` (default `2000`) and, optionally,\n`--diar-max-speakers` (no limit by default)."],[220,"`--dry-run` shows the per-table counts (imported, skipped, unsupported) without\nwriting anything. Add `--json` for a structured report."],[183,"`--engine`: `app-default` (default; the **Transcription model** chosen in\nSettings → **Speech**, which is Parakeet unless you changed it), `parakeet`,\n`nemotron`, `whisper`, or `moss` (the Detailed model; about 2 GB to download\nthe first time)."],[185,"`--format`: `text` (default), `transcript` (timestamped lines), or `json`."],[184,"`--language`: a language hint for `nemotron` or `whisper`, e.g. `en`, `it`.\nWithout it, Whisper uses the language set in the app."],[202,"`--local`: uses the smaller context budget meant for local models."],[199,"`--model`: pick a model. Required for `openaiCompatible`, `lmstudio`, `omlx`\nand `nativ`; other providers fall back to a conservative default."],[188,"`--no-save`: transcribe without adding the result to your library."],[186,"`--processing`: `raw` keeps the engine's text; `clean` rebuilds punctuation\nand applies your **Word corrections** (Settings → **Speech** → **Your words &\nphrases**), as the app does for meetings. The default, `app-default`, uses a\npreference older versions stored and otherwise behaves like `raw`. Pass\n`clean` to match the app. With `--diarize`, the output is speaker-labeled\ntext and this option is ignored."],[225,"`0`: success."],[226,"`1`: a runtime failure (network, provider, transcription), including a\nmeeting ID or title that matches nothing."],[227,"`2`: an invalid value (unknown engine, format or provider, a missing\n`--model`), a missing or empty input file, or an ID prefix or title that is\nambiguous or too short."],[228,"`64`: the command line itself couldn't be parsed (an unknown option or a\nmissing argument). These errors are printed as plain text, even with\n`--json`."],[218,"`ai-usage` shows how much each feature used the AI today: calls, prompt and\ngenerated tokens, and time. `--day YYYY-MM-DD` picks another day, `--days N`\nthe most recent N days with any use, and `--json` prints JSON."],[213,"`calendar upcoming` lists the events your calendar rules match in the next 7\ndays."],[217,"`config` reads and writes settings."],[173,"`everpaper-cli` lives inside the app at\n`/Applications/Everpaper.app/Contents/MacOS/everpaper-cli` (a `hindsight-cli`\nalias sits next to it for old scripts). To run it as a bare command, link it\nonto your `PATH`:"],[214,"`health` checks paths, the database, models and calendar access."],[216,"`history` lists and searches saved transcriptions."],[191,"`models download` fetches `nemotron`, `whisper` or `moss` ahead of time;\nParakeet downloads itself on first use. `models warm-up` loads an engine so the\nfirst transcription starts fast (`--engine` defaults to `nemotron`; it may\ndownload first). `models clear` deletes the speech models Everpaper downloaded\nfor transcription, including the ones it uses for meetings (Parakeet, Nemotron\nand the Detailed model). The app downloads them again when it next needs them,\nso your next meeting may start with a download."],[204,"`native check` sends one prompt through Everpaper Native and prints the model,\nthe reply, token counts and latency. `native models` lists the installed models."],[212,"`prompts run \"Meeting Memo\" <meeting> --provider …` runs a template and saves\nthe result to the meeting (`prompts list|show|add|set|delete|restore-defaults`\nmanage them)."],[215,"`vocab` manages word corrections and saved phrases."],[1316,"⌘K, then **Check for updates**"],[768,"1.0.0"],[767,"1.0.1"],[758,"1.0.10"],[757,"1.0.11"],[756,"1.0.12"],[766,"1.0.2"],[765,"1.0.3"],[764,"1.0.4"],[763,"1.0.5"],[762,"1.0.6"],[761,"1.0.7"],[760,"1.0.8"],[759,"1.0.9"],[755,"1.1.0"],[754,"1.1.1"],[738,"1.10.0"],[737,"1.11.0"],[736,"1.12.0"],[735,"1.12.1"],[734,"1.12.2"],[733,"1.13.0"],[732,"1.14.0"],[731,"1.15.0"],[730,"1.15.1"],[729,"1.16.0"],[753,"1.2.0"],[752,"1.3.0"],[751,"1.3.1"],[750,"1.4.0"],[749,"1.4.1"],[748,"1.5.0"],[747,"1.5.1"],[746,"1.5.2"],[745,"1.6.0"],[744,"1.7.0"],[743,"1.8.0"],[742,"1.8.1"],[741,"1.8.2"],[740,"1.9.0"],[739,"1.9.1"],[337,"1\\. Your memory"],[698,"2.0.0-b100 — beta"],[697,"2.0.0-b101 — beta"],[728,"2.0.0-b70 — beta"],[727,"2.0.0-b71 — beta"],[726,"2.0.0-b72 — beta"],[725,"2.0.0-b73 — beta"],[724,"2.0.0-b74 — beta"],[723,"2.0.0-b75 — beta"],[722,"2.0.0-b76 · b77 — beta"],[721,"2.0.0-b78 — beta"],[720,"2.0.0-b79 — beta"],[719,"2.0.0-b80 — beta"],[715,"2.0.0-b80-event — beta"],[718,"2.0.0-b81 — beta"],[717,"2.0.0-b82 — beta"],[716,"2.0.0-b83 — beta"],[714,"2.0.0-b84 — beta"],[713,"2.0.0-b85-event — beta"],[712,"2.0.0-b86-event — beta"],[711,"2.0.0-b87-event — beta"],[710,"2.0.0-b88-event — beta"],[709,"2.0.0-b89-event — beta"],[708,"2.0.0-b90-event — beta"],[707,"2.0.0-b91-event — beta"],[706,"2.0.0-b92-event — beta"],[705,"2.0.0-b93-event — beta"],[704,"2.0.0-b94-event — beta"],[703,"2.0.0-b95-event — beta"],[702,"2.0.0-b96-nemotron — beta"],[701,"2.0.0-b97-journal — beta"],[700,"2.0.0-b98 — beta"],[699,"2.0.0-b99 — beta"],[338,"2\\. Daily history"],[71,"24 GB of memory or more (suggested there)"],[74,"24 GB or more"],[339,"3\\. Access"],[77,"32 GB or more"],[340,"4\\. Private summaries"],[341,"5\\. Your setup"],[250,"A **MOMENTS** count. Press **Mark Moment** to flag something worth\nremembering as it happens; you can add a short note to a moment, too."],[248,"A **RECORDING** indicator with an elapsed clock."],[1192,"A bigger delete control, and clearer labels distinguishing asking the whole book from\nchatting with a single day."],[1201,"A broad polish pass: more reliable meeting status, far wider accessibility, and a\nfaster, more consistent interface."],[389,"A counter such as **3 of 4 ready** sits at the top, and a bookmark appears\nonly when everything you chose is really ready. **YOUR FIRST MOMENT**\nsuggests where to begin. Press **Open Everpaper**."],[1138,"A dedicated embeddings model picker for Folder Context RAG — and three fixes the adversarial code review caught before they reached you."],[1094,"A design release. Every page got a pass from an expert review of the whole interface — smarter suggestions, honest insights, readable type, and dozens of paper cuts gone."],[1256,"A few gigabytes of free disk space for the speech model, which downloads once\nduring first-time setup."],[857,"A fix for a crash when you start a meeting while your workday is being\nrecorded."],[1208,"A fix so a finished day always shows up where you expect it."],[1247,"A floating pill and a live panel with **Notes**, a speaker-labeled\n**Transcript**, and an **Ask** tab."],[1106,"A focused reliability release for reminders — the Today rail's suggested to-dos and calendar meeting alerts."],[1216,"A formatting hotfix for AI answers."],[486,"A Mac with no microphone records system audio only. Switching speakers or\nheadphones during a meeting, Bluetooth included, doesn't lose the recording."],[1125,"A more AI-driven Second Brain — pick your own embedding model, smarter suggested to-dos, and meaning-aware file indexing — plus a wave of reliability fixes across reminders, your days, and the Second Brain."],[777,"A new look, a launcher on ⌥Space, Dictate on fn, and a journal that knows what\nday it is."],[654,"A note on where this runs"],[1239,"A packaging release focused on a smoother, more reliable first launch."],[1249,"A page that reads across every day you've captured, links the things that keep\nrecurring, and forms plain-language **beliefs** about your focus. It moves\nbetween **Chat**, **Insights**, **Network**, and **Export**. The beliefs,\nInsights, and Network are computed locally and work with no AI provider\nconnected; recall grounds every chat answer in your real history with citations\nback to the moment each claim came from."],[1070,"A polish release: reading comfort, tidier lists, and small consistency fixes across the book."],[1091,"A quick fix on the heels of 1.9.0."],[1102,"A quick follow-up to the file-indexing rework: live status you can actually watch, and proper patience with local AI models that are still loading."],[1181,"A real privacy hardening for AI vision:"],[500,"A recording stopped within its first five seconds isn't kept (&#x2A;That wasn't long\nenough to keep.*). If Everpaper quits or your Mac restarts mid-meeting, the\naudio isn't lost: the Meetings page lists it under **Needs attention**, marked\n*Stopped mid-recording*, with a **Recover** button. Recovery re-transcribes the\nwhole recording and can take several minutes."],[1230,"A reliability release for meeting recording — especially your first one after\ninstalling."],[249,"A running **FRAMES** count (screenshots taken so far)."],[1244,"A running record of your day, woven from periodic screen capture read with\non-device OCR (Apple Vision). Turn between days with the left and right arrows,\nlet the red Ribbon bring you back to today, and use **Ask the book** on any page\nto pose a question about your day or your history. Screenshots and the journal\ndatabase never leave your Mac."],[501,"A saved meeting opens on four tabs: **Transcript** (⌘1),\n**Overview** (⌘2, with the summary and templates), **Ask**\n(⌘3, to keep asking about the meeting) and **Speakers**\n(⌘4). The toolbar has **Copy**, **Export** (Plain Text, Markdown,\nSRT subtitles, WebVTT or JSON), **Audio** (saves the recording as an .m4a file)\nand **More** (to-dos, re-analysis, &#x2A;*Rename…**, **Name from Calendar Event**).\nPress ⌘F to find words in the transcript. In the Library, each row\nalso has &#x2A;*Save Transcript…** and &#x2A;*Save Audio…**."],[257,"A screenshot of each display at your interval, read on this Mac"],[925,"A short introduction before setup, and questions about a meeting become\nconversations you can come back to."],[1233,"A small reliability release for first-run setup."],[1170,"A smarter privacy boundary for on-device AI — and a more consistent feel throughout."],[1006,"A sweep through everything the app wasn't saying out loud."],[263,"A timeline of apps and windows. No screenshots"],[79,"about 1.9 GB"],[82,"about 2.3 GB"],[73,"about 4 GB"],[70,"about 6.9 GB"],[76,"about 8.2 GB"],[450,"After a meeting"],[659,"After an answer, **Correct** sets the record straight for future answers, and\n**Continue in Memory** (⌘Return) carries the conversation into\nMemory's Ask tab."],[209,"Agents get people, files, links and connections from your journal, with\nsecrets removed (names, email addresses and phone numbers are kept), and never\nscreenshots. Full-text search of your screen text needs a second switch,\n&#x2A;*…including raw screen text (full-text search)**, which appears once the first\none is on."],[596,"AI & summaries → Access from other AI apps"],[623,"AI & summaries → Files you include"],[608,"AI & summaries → Your memory & summaries"],[611,"AI & summaries → Your memory & summaries"],[587,"AI & summaries → Your memory & summaries (and Privacy)"],[527,"AI is opt-in"],[1,"AI on your Mac"],[581,"All of these are in **Settings** unless noted."],[409,"Also in this beta"],[357,"Always run Everpaper from the Applications folder, not from the disk image or\nDownloads. Otherwise macOS can't keep the Screen Recording permission."],[952,"An early tester build of Computer History."],[1184,"And a sweep of reliability and polish: the recording control no longer lets *Discard* look like the main action; a failed Second Brain question now tells you instead of hanging; the \"relaunch to apply Screen Recording\" state stays visible until you act on it; a new multi-step progress bar in setup; and consistent ask fields, status dots, and delete confirmations throughout."],[533,"Answers are checked"],[153,"Any change you make takes effect immediately. Switch back to **Off** and\nEverpaper stops watching for upcoming meetings: no reminders, no countdowns, no\nbackground timer. Change the start prompt, the eligibility or the excluded\ncalendars and the next reminder or countdown follows the new rules. If a\ncountdown is on screen when you change a setting, it closes."],[44,"any endpoint you enter"],[88,"Anything you're waiting for, such as an answer you asked for, goes first.\nBackground work waits its turn. Work nobody is waiting on, such as catch-up\nwrite-ups and bringing older history up to date for search, waits for a quiet\nmoment: it holds off while another app uses the microphone, while you record a\nmeeting, while an app is in full screen, when the Mac is hot or short on\nmemory, in Low Power Mode, on battery, and while you're using the Mac. A\nlocked screen counts as a quiet moment. The running notes hold off only while\nyou record a meeting, in Low Power Mode, when the Mac is hot or very short on\nmemory, and on battery if **Reduce work on battery** is on. Work with a\ndeadline still gets done in time."],[561,"API keys"],[1255,"Apple Silicon (M-series) — the on-device speech model runs on the Neural\nEngine."],[353,"Apply at everpaper.app/beta. If you're\nselected, you'll hear back within 48 hours."],[1289,"As you work, Everpaper reads the captured text and pulls out concrete things:\nemail addresses and phone numbers, files, websites (on sites like GitHub, each\nrepository counts as its own thing) and ticket references like `#318`.\nExtraction runs on this Mac on text that has already had secrets removed.\nEveryday plumbing — sign-in pages, analytics, content networks, and\nboilerplate files such as a project's readme — is filtered out. Things that\nkeep appearing together become connections."],[1261,"Ask"],[448,"Ask during a meeting"],[1258,"Ask questions across everything Everpaper has kept, see what it has learned about your work, explore connections, and export it."],[644,"Ask your book a question and get an answer drawn from your own days, meetings and files, found by what they mean, with a link back to the exact moment it came from."],[647,"Ask your memory: a question across every day"],[653,"Asking about a single meeting"],[89,"At launch, the Reflection and catch-up write-ups wait for the first quiet\nmoment. When Everpaper starts at login without opening a window, they wait\nuntil you open it, or for a quiet moment about 15 minutes later."],[494,"At the foot of the live transcript, type in &#x2A;*Ask about what's been said…** to\nask your AI provider about the conversation so far and your notes. Without a\nprovider you'll see *Ask needs an AI provider* and an **Open Settings** button.\nThe conversation carries over to the saved meeting's **Ask** tab."],[446,"Audio and meeting settings"],[1298,"Automatic updates (after launch)"],[562,"Bearer tokens"],[626,"Before a Memory answer is shown, Everpaper checks each sentence. If it names a\nperson, file or other named thing that isn't in your captured history, or cites\na source that doesn't exist, that sentence is removed. A name that is only a\nclose match is kept but flagged. This catches most invented names, but it can't\njudge every fact, so open the cited sources before you rely on an answer."],[1280,"Before an answer is saved, Everpaper checks it against the sources it was given\nand removes sentences it can't support. The check is strict but not perfect:\nit can occasionally remove a correct detail or miss an invented name. When an\nanswer matters, open its sources."],[558,"Before any text is sent to a provider, Everpaper runs an offline,\non-device pass that scrubs secrets out of it. This runs both at capture time\n(before anything is written to your journal) and again before every outbound AI\ncall. It recognizes:"],[687,"Before macOS 27, search by meaning covers text; screenshots and meetings stay\nsearchable by their words."],[305,"Before you export"],[682,"Before you've typed anything, Recall shows **You keep coming back to** — the\npeople, sites, and files that recur across your days, each with how many days\nit spans and how many times you've seen it. Click one to turn it into a search."],[467,"Before your first meeting, never during one, macOS also asks for **System\nAudio Recording**, a lighter way for Everpaper to hear the call. It isn't\nrequired: if you decline, Everpaper records the call through screen capture as\nbefore and tells you so. Check or change it under **System audio route** in\n**Settings → Meetings → During a meeting** (**Allow now**, or **Check again**\nafter a decline)."],[520,"Best AI meeting assistants: how bot and bot-free tools compare."],[1294,"Best AI memory apps for Mac: what each one remembers and where data goes."],[329,"Best Obsidian alternatives: where the Obsidian vault export fits."],[55,"Between uses the engine stays loaded until your next journal review is due\n(about 13 minutes with the default 10-minute review), so each review finds it\nready. On Macs with 16 GB of memory or less, or when memory is tight, it\ncloses about a minute after use. While you record a meeting it steps aside\nright away, unless the As we go digest is running on it. Stopping an answer\ndoesn't restart it."],[322,"Both are derived from the same snapshot Memory's **Connections** tab shows:\nnodes are the things you keep returning to, and edges are the links between\nthings that keep appearing together (weighted by how strong the connection is)."],[284,"Both are in **Settings → Daily history → Capture schedule**. Nothing is\ncaptured while the display is asleep, and Detailed screen history's extra\nimages also stop while the screen is locked."],[868,"Both are off by default, a recording never starts a model download, and saved\nmeetings stay compatible."],[219,"Bring data over from the old Hindsight app (the same import as Settings →\n**General** → **Setup & data** → &#x2A;*Import from Hindsight (legacy)**). Quit Hindsight first. Your\noriginals are never changed, and nothing imports on its own. Use\n`--legacy-root <folder>` if Hindsight's data isn't in its usual place."],[172,"By default the CLI uses the app's shared database. Pass `--database <path>` on\nmost commands to point at a different one."],[189,"By default the result is saved to your library so it appears in the app."],[325,"By default the vault and graph are written inside Everpaper's own folder in\nyour user Library. After an export runs, a **Reveal in Finder** button appears\nnext to it, the quickest way to jump to the files. The status line below the buttons confirms\nwhat was written (for example, &#x2A;Exported 12 days · 34 people · 18 links · 9\nfiles.* for the vault, or &#x2A;Exported graph · 60 nodes · 142 edges (GraphML +\nMermaid).* for the graph)."],[110,"Calendar Auto-Start"],[151,"Calendar data never stops a recording. If a meeting runs long, Everpaper keeps\nrecording until you stop it yourself."],[296,"Capture and text recognition happen on this Mac. Text leaves it only if you set\nup an AI that sends it, or let another AI app read your journal; see\nAI on your Mac."],[370,"Capture starts only when you start a workday journal. The\nDay Journal page compares the modes."],[475,"Change ⇧⌘M."],[660,"Change the shortcut in **Settings → General → The Concordance**. If another\napp has taken it, use **Go → Summon the Concordance**."],[12,"Change, test or remove"],[118,"Changes apply right away"],[345,"Changing things later"],[1299,"Checking manually"],[582,"Choice"],[531,"Choices and their defaults"],[113,"Choose a behavior"],[864,"Choose Everyday or Detailed meetings, and try live speaker labels."],[503,"Choose how meetings are transcribed in **Settings → Speech → Meetings**.\n**Everyday** (the default) is fast: live captions and speaker labels as you\ntalk. **Detailed** re-listens to the whole call after you stop, so results take\nlonger but are more thorough; it needs a one-time download of about 2 GB. If\nyou pick Detailed before it's set up, meetings use Everyday until it is."],[232,"Choose what your day keeps"],[1221,"Clearer day navigation."],[123,"Click **Grant Calendar Access**. macOS asks once for full calendar access,\nthe only level that lets an app read events; Everpaper only reads them."],[1287,"Click a surprising connection, or a link in the graph, to see why the two go\ntogether: how often they were seen together and when last, the captures where\nboth appear (**Seen together**), and, with an AI model connected, one sentence\nexplaining the link. If the two are really the same thing, choose **Merge**."],[674,"Click any source to open that frame: the original screen, at the moment it was\ncaptured, so you can verify the answer with your own eyes. Every `[#N]` in the\nanswer maps to a row in the list. Answers can still get a detail wrong, so open\nthe sources when it matters."],[147,"Click anywhere on the countdown, or its ✕, to cancel. That meeting won't try\nagain."],[854,"Click details are still recorded, and capture modes and saved data are\nunchanged. The crash isn't proven fixed yet; if clicking ever feels slow,\nreport the app you were in and roughly when."],[681,"Click one to seed the search and see every matching moment."],[487,"Click the floating pill to open the meeting panel. There are no tabs: your\nnotes are on the left and the live transcript is on the right."],[47,"cloud"],[1308,"Coming from a Hindsight-branded release? The old update feed has been\nretired, so it won't offer Everpaper. Quit Hindsight, then install Everpaper\nfrom your beta link (or apply at\neverpaper.app/beta) into Applications. It\nuses the same data folder, so your history is kept. Then move the old\nHindsight app to the Trash."],[156,"Command line"],[529,"Computer History"],[600,"Computer History (**Record clicks and text**)"],[944,"Computer History arrives, with a new Adaptive mode, a History that reads as\nwork, and a clearer first run."],[568,"Computer History is an optional record of what you do on your Mac, beyond\nwhat's on screen. It's offered in every history mode except Screen history, and\nstays off until you turn it on (**Computer History events & text** in setup,\n**Record clicks and text** in **Settings → Daily history**)."],[936,"Computer History now records what you do by default once you opt in, and\nDetailed screen history can't record less than Screen history."],[1081,"Concordance gets a real memory of your meetings, and outside transcripts can join your library."],[112,"Connect a calendar"],[165,"Connect another AI app (MCP)"],[1265,"Connections"],[1279,"Conversations are saved: **Conversations** reopens one, and **New\nconversation** starts a fresh one. An empty conversation offers up to three\n**Try asking** questions drawn from your own history."],[602,"Daily history"],[229,"Day Journal"],[769,"Day Journal"],[1134,"Debugging tools for the embeddings provider — so you can tell when your model isn't loaded and fix it without reading server logs."],[583,"Default"],[534,"Deleting what Everpaper keeps"],[265,"Detailed screen history keeps going while you read or watch without typing or\nclicking: at most one extra image every 30 seconds, and only when what's on\nscreen changed. Grant Accessibility for it in **Settings → General → Mac\npermissions**; the timed screenshots work without it."],[580,"Detailed screen history takes its window images in browsers with or without\nthe extension, reading the page address through Accessibility, and still\nleaves out skipped sites and private windows. A screenshot\nshows your whole display, so a skipped app, site or private window that is\nvisible behind another window can still appear. Use\n**Pause**, or Activity only, for sensitive\nwork. The related moments on Today and in meetings never start from a skipped\napp, a skipped site or a private window. For Computer History, use\n&#x2A;*Interaction source policy → Manage…** to exclude apps and websites."],[146,"Do nothing, and recording starts when the countdown ends."],[67,"Download"],[85,"During a Detailed meeting on a Mac with less than 32 GB, Gemma 4 E4B steps\naside to leave room for the meeting model. While it does, the As we go digest\nis skipped, and the summary and journal wait until the meeting ends. If you\nset Gemma up yourself on a Mac with less than 24 GB, it steps aside during\nevery meeting."],[236,"During meetings"],[1317,"During the private beta a check always finds nothing, because betas aren't\npublished to the update feed. To see whether a newer beta exists, look at the\nrelease notes. Your copy's version is in **Settings →\nAbout**."],[358,"During the private beta Everpaper does not update itself. Each new build\narrives as a new link, and you install it over the old copy; your history,\nsettings and models stay in place. See Updates & Support."],[1278,"Each answer lists its **Sources**. Open one to check the original screen, the\nmeeting passage (**Transcript · 12:34**) or the file. When an answer is a\nnumbered list, each point has **Correct** (save the right version for future\nanswers) and **Forget** (stop using that claim)."],[354,"Each build reaches you as a private download link to a disk image named\nafter its version. Please don't share the link."],[377,"Each button asks macOS directly. It reads &#x2A;*Waiting for macOS…** while the\nsystem dialog is open, then **Allowed**. If the request isn't granted, it\nturns into **Open System Settings** instead of asking again."],[310,"Each day becomes a daily note, and the people, sites, and files you\nencountered become their own notes, joined together with `[[wikilinks]]` so the\nwhole thing browses as a connected web."],[567,"Each match is replaced inline with a placeholder like `[REDACTED:api_key]`\nbefore the text is saved or sent to an AI. Screenshots are images, so a secret\nthat was on screen still shows in them, and only patterns like those above are\ncaught. The redaction pass runs entirely on your Mac and makes no network calls\nof its own."],[178,"Each meeting also keeps **notes** and saved **results** (the output of prompts\nyou ran on it):"],[137,"Each reminder fires once per meeting. If your Mac is asleep or busy at the exact\nmoment, Everpaper still catches the reminder for a short window afterward so a\nlate tick won't make you miss it."],[622,"EmbeddingGemma-2, on this Mac"],[616,"empty until you add something"],[776,"Entries marked **beta** are pre-release builds shared with testers ahead of a\npublic version. Automatic updates never offer them."],[1262,"Episodes"],[1281,"Episodes is paused in this beta. It was designed to summarize every ten minutes\nof activity and group the summaries into six-hour chapters, but this build\ndoesn't write new ones. On a new install the tab stays empty. Your day is still\nwritten up in Timeline."],[107,"Evening entries can condense busy days and leave out some meeting details."],[401,"Everpaper b101: the launcher, Dictate and clipboard history."],[694,"Everpaper b101: what the launcher and Dictate do."],[281,"Everpaper can also pause on its own:"],[120,"Everpaper can watch your calendar so you never miss the start of a meeting. When\nan upcoming event matches your rules, Everpaper either drops a quiet reminder or\nopens a short, cancellable countdown and starts recording for you."],[544,"Everpaper goes online only for these:"],[65,"Everpaper installs the oMLX server (from GitHub) and the model you choose (from\nHugging Face), then runs them for you. In setup's advanced options, pick a\nmodel and press **Install and prepare**. The one marked **SUGGESTED** is the\nrecommendation for this Mac. The choices:"],[403,"Everpaper is a private memory of your workdays and meetings on your Mac — Today, Meetings, Timeline, Library and Memory — processed on this Mac by default."],[413,"Everpaper is a private memory of your workdays and meetings, on your Mac. Start\na workday journal and it keeps a searchable history of what you worked on.\nRecord a meeting and it keeps the audio, a transcript and a summary. Later, ask\nit what you need and it answers with the source."],[538,"Everpaper is built to stay on your Mac. Capture, transcription, the knowledge\ngraph, and your whole journal database live locally, and nothing is sent to an AI\nunless you connect a provider and — for cloud providers — explicitly turn cloud\nanalysis on. This page explains exactly what stays put, what can leave, and how\nto control it."],[415,"Everpaper is in private beta. Getting started explains how\nto join and install it."],[347,"Everpaper is in private beta. This page covers joining, installing, and the\nshort setup that runs the first time you open it."],[435,"Everpaper is on-device by default:"],[775,"Everpaper is the new name for Hindsight — same app, same data, same\npermissions. Entries below from before the rename describe Hindsight as it\nwas branded at the time and are left as originally written."],[164,"Everpaper Native"],[5,"Everpaper Native (macOS 27)"],[919,"Everpaper Native answers finish when the text does."],[466,"Everpaper needs **Screen & System Audio Recording** access (that is how macOS\nlets it hear the call) and, unless you chose System Audio Only, **Microphone**\naccess. If one is missing, it opens the right pane of System Settings. Keep\nEverpaper in your Applications folder, or macOS won't remember the grant."],[1318,"Everpaper never interrupts a recording to update. Update checks are paused\nwhile a meeting is recording, and an already-downloaded update will not\nrelaunch the app mid-recording. Stop the recording and the update can go\nahead. If you press &#x2A;*Check for Updates…** in Settings during a recording,\nEverpaper shows &#x2A;Finish recording before requesting another Everpaper release\ncheck.* From the menus, the check simply doesn't run. Try again once the\nrecording has ended."],[552,"Everpaper never talks to an AI provider unless you set one up:"],[154,"Everpaper only reads your calendar. It keeps no copy and never uploads it. Once\naccess is granted, Everpaper also uses your calendar after each recording,\nwhatever the behavior: a meeting that still has its default name takes the\ntitle of the event it overlapped, and in a one-to-one call the other speaker can\nbe named from the invite (*from the calendar invite* on the **Speakers** tab).\nThose details travel with the meeting like any title or name you type, for\nexample to an AI provider you set up. To stop all of this, remove Everpaper's\naccess in **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Calendars**."],[15,"Everpaper records, transcribes, reads your screen and keeps your journal\nwithout any AI. A language model adds the written parts: the running notes and\nevening entry of your day, decisions and commitments, meeting summaries and the\nAs we go digest, and answers when you ask a question. You can run that model on\nthis Mac, connect one you already use, or use a cloud provider with your\nconsent. You can also go without: in setup, choose **Set up later**, or\n**Choose advanced options → No AI for now**."],[511,"Everpaper remembers a voice only after it has a name. To stop it, turn off\n**Remember voices between meetings** in **Settings → Privacy → Voices, storage\n& journal controls**; that deletes every stored voice. If two labels are the\nsame person, the merge card asks whether **They're the same person**."],[683,"Everpaper searches your days, meetings and files with a search model that runs\non this Mac, **EmbeddingGemma-2**. It finds a moment by what it was about, even\nwhen you describe it in other words, or in another language, than the ones on\nscreen. Exact names and phrases still match first. It's what Ask the book,\n&#x2A;*Ask your memory…**, the Concordance and Memory's **Ask** use to find their\nsources."],[170,"Everpaper ships with a companion command-line tool, `everpaper-cli`, that talks\nto the same database as the app. Anything you record, transcribe, or learn in\nthe app is visible from the terminal, and anything the CLI writes shows up in\nthe app. It is handy for scripting, exporting, and feeding your transcripts into\nother tools."],[423,"Everpaper treats your history as a book. That's where the small second names\nunder three of these places come from (The Days, The Archive, Second Brain),\nand why the Index files the beliefs Memory has formed under Commonplace Book."],[157,"everpaper-cli is a companion tool that shares the app's database — list and export meetings, transcribe files on-device, manage speech models, inspect Memory, connect other AI apps (MCP), and run LLM tasks from the terminal."],[1309,"Everpaper's update checks are already built in. Choose how they work in\n**Settings → General → Updates** (⌘,):"],[273,"Every **10 minutes** by default (**Settings → Daily history → Review new\nactivity every**: 10, 15, 30 or 60 minutes), your AI reviews what's new and\nadds to the day's running notes. The notes build up through the day, and get\none full tidy-up in the evening."],[557,"Every AI feature in the app passes the same check before anything is sent: if\nno provider is configured the call is declined, an on-device or trusted\nprovider is allowed, and a cloud provider is allowed only with your consent.\nJournal text, meeting transcripts, chat questions and notes all go through it.\nTwo things work differently: &#x2A;*Allow external agents (MCP)** lets another AI\napp read your journal (see below), and the\ncommand line's AI commands send text to the provider you name on each call\n(see Command line). See\nAI on your Mac for every option."],[80,"every Mac (suggested below 24 GB)"],[83,"every Mac; best for shorter meetings"],[451,"Everyday or Detailed"],[977,"Everything else — recording, transcription, journal — is unchanged from\nb79 and continues to use the existing engines regardless of this setting."],[922,"Everything else is as in b86."],[414,"Everything you capture is stored and processed on this Mac by default. Nothing\ngoes to a cloud service unless you connect one and separately allow it."],[907,"Existing journals aren't rewritten automatically. How good the writing is\nstill depends on your local model."],[888,"Existing journals aren't rewritten. The writing still depends on your local\nmodel and isn't yet as good as it used to be."],[169,"Exit codes"],[299,"Export"],[1266,"Export"],[302,"Export a graph"],[301,"Export an Obsidian vault"],[328,"Export is fully local. It reuses only data Everpaper has already captured on\nyour Mac, and nothing is sent anywhere. Secrets such as card numbers, keys and\none-time codes are removed, but names, email addresses, links and file paths\nare kept. They are what make the vault useful. Keep the exported folder as\nprivate as your journal. The files are yours to move, edit, and back up\nhowever you like."],[326,"Export needs something to export, so journal a few days first. With\nnothing captured yet, the status line reads &#x2A;Exported 0 days…*."],[307,"Export turns what Everpaper has already captured into open, portable files you\ncan browse, link, and visualize in other tools. Nothing new leaves your machine\n— export only writes out the on-device data you can already see in the app.\nExport is a one-way snapshot you can re-run any time."],[1120,"Export your meeting transcripts, get real speakers out of single-microphone recordings, and watch — and pause — file indexing while it runs."],[303,"Exporting meetings"],[624,"External agents get people, files, links and connections, with secrets removed\n(names, email addresses and phone numbers are kept), and never screenshots.\nWhat an agent reads is then handled by that app and the AI it uses, which may\nbe a cloud service. **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal** doesn't apply to\nit, so turn MCP on only for apps you trust. **AI refinement of insights** lets\nyour AI tidy the beliefs and topics in Memory; turn it off to keep the plain\nversion. Like everything else, it uses only the AI you chose, under the same\nrules."],[1113,"File indexing got a ground-up rework — dramatically faster and easier on your Mac — and your indexed files now back up the Second Brain's answers directly."],[13,"Files and other AI apps"],[1176,"Find any day in your history at a glance, and never lose one to an accidental delete."],[343,"Finding your way"],[404,"Finding your way"],[1303,"Finish or stop any recording, then quit Everpaper."],[318,"Finished days are included, and so is a day you've stopped but haven't reviewed\nyet. Every note is plain Markdown — no lock-in. Re-running the export refreshes\nthe vault in place."],[291,"Finished days live in **Timeline** in the sidebar. Each page shows the **Day\nSummary**, what you did and when, your captures, and your notes. The side rail\nlists the day's **Decisions** and **Commitments** (with AI), people and\nprojects, open threads, and your rhythm. &#x2A;*Ask this day…** answers questions\nabout that day only. To remove a day, use **Delete this day and all its\nevidence**; you can undo it from Today right after."],[956,"First-run setup now asks which intelligence route you want instead of deciding\nfor you."],[335,"First-time setup"],[1052,"First-time setup, rebuilt — the front door now matches the book behind it."],[850,"Fixes for a crash and a hang linked to recording your clicks."],[1218,"Fixes meeting transcription."],[179,"For `notes set` and `results add`, pass `-` instead of the text to read it from\nstandard input."],[636,"For a cloud provider, paste your **API key**. For a local provider, you can\nusually leave the key blank."],[566,"For cloud providers, and endpoints you haven't marked trusted, Everpaper also\nreplaces email addresses, phone numbers and names it detects, for example\n`[REDACTED:email]`."],[1319,"Found a bug, or have an idea? **Help → Everpaper Help** or **Help → View on\nGitHub** opens the project on GitHub:\ngithub.com/iannellomarco/everpaper.\nOpen an issue there to report a problem or request a feature."],[1290,"From what recurs, Everpaper keeps a short list of one-line beliefs: people you\ndeal with, projects, tools and sites, files you return to, rhythms in your\nwork, and open loops (a file or ticket you touched a couple of times and then\nleft for about a week). They're worked out on your Mac without AI, grow\nstronger when they recur and fade after quiet days. Less certain ones read as\n&#x2A;*(likely)*&#x2A; or &#x2A;*(tentative)**. Observations the Reflection keeps making can\nbecome traits, which you'll see under **What I'm picking up about you**."],[69,"Gemma 4 E4B"],[103,"Gemma 4 E4B in Everpaper Native hasn't been tested on a real 16 GB Mac yet."],[815,"Gemma 4 runs inside Everpaper on macOS 27."],[411,"Get started"],[331,"Getting started"],[344,"Granting Screen Recording on recent macOS"],[180,"Have a transcript from somewhere else? Import a WebVTT file as a meeting with\nno audio:"],[440,"Head to Getting started to join the private beta, install\nEverpaper and walk through setup."],[455,"Hiding speakers"],[514,"Hiding survives re-analysis. It changes only what this meeting's transcript\nshows: **Copy**, **Export**, **Audio** and the meeting's Summary still include\neveryone. To remove a recording, delete the meeting."],[523,"How Everpaper keeps your data on-device by default, how optional cloud AI is gated by consent and redacted before it leaves your Mac, and where to configure a provider and keys."],[1268,"How far back it remembers"],[526,"How long it's kept"],[238,"How long your days are kept"],[1259,"How ready it is"],[648,"How the answer is built"],[197,"How the key is resolved, in order: `--api-key` (a literal — discouraged), then\n`--api-key-env` (the name of an environment variable holding the key), then the\nconventional variable for the provider (for example `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,\n`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `GEMINI_API_KEY`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`). `ollama`, `lmstudio`,\n`omlx` and `nativ` need no key; if your LM Studio, oMLX or Nativ server uses a\ntoken, set `LM_API_TOKEN`, `OMLX_API_TOKEN` or `NATIV_API_TOKEN`."],[1296,"How to install a new beta, how automatic updates will work after launch, and where to report problems."],[675,"If Everpaper can't use an AI for this, Recall still works. Instead of prose, it\nreturns **cards** — each one a specific match (a phone number, an email, a\nlink, a file you saw) carrying the same time-and-place stamp, and a click opens\nthe frame it came from. Recording and search never need AI; the answer style\njust changes."],[673,"If Everpaper may use your AI (an on-device or trusted private provider, or a\ncloud provider with **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal** turned on), the\nanswer streams back as prose, written only from the days and frames that match\nyour question. Under the answer is a **Sources** list. Each source is the exact\nframe the answer drew from, shown as its time, the app, and the window title —\nfor example:"],[398,"If the entry doesn't respond, remove it from the list with &#x2A;*−*&#x2A;, add\nEverpaper from your Applications folder again with &#x2A;*+**, then relaunch. If\nsetup says Everpaper is running outside Applications, move it there first:\nmacOS can't keep the grant otherwise."],[86,"If you already run LM Studio or Ollama, Everpaper uses a chat model it already\nhas; it never downloads into another app. On Macs with 24 GB or more, the\nsuggested model is Gemma 4 E4B: `google/gemma-4-e4b` in LM Studio,\n`gemma4:e4b` in Ollama. **OpenAI-Compatible** works with any server that speaks\nthe OpenAI API: enter its base URL and model."],[56,"If you already use Everpaper Native with Qwen on a Mac with 16 GB or more, a\none-time card, **Gemma 4 E4B writes a better journal**, offers the switch. The\nsame offer stays in Settings as **Get Gemma 4 E4B**. Qwen stays on your Mac\nuntil you choose **Remove Qwen3 4B** on the card, or **Remove** in Settings."],[382,"If you chose meetings, the same step also prepares meeting transcription and\nlive speaker labels. **Prepare on this Mac** also sets up the search model\n(see Search by meaning). Nothing downloads until\nyou press it, and you can keep going while it runs; the search model carries\non preparing in the background after you move on. **Cancel preparation**\nstops it."],[144,"If you have several calendars, expand **Calendars** to exclude specific ones: a\npersonal calendar, say, while keeping your work calendar. New calendars are\nincluded by default."],[860,"If you stop the workday while the meeting continues, Today shows the day's\nwriting and review status."],[287,"If you upgraded from an earlier build that already had journal data, the\nswitch starts off and only images are removed. Meetings are not affected."],[266,"If you used the earlier **Events + focused fallback** mode, Everpaper moved you\nto Detailed screen history. You can pick another mode at any time."],[211,"If your agent reports *Enable it in Everpaper → Settings → Journal*, the switch\nis at Settings → **AI & summaries** → **Access from other AI apps** → &#x2A;*Allow\nexternal agents (MCP)**."],[167,"Import"],[1275,"In **Activity only** (Settings: Events only), Everpaper takes no screenshots,\nso **Frames processed** stays at 0. The page never reaches **Ready**, and no\nReflection is written. Ask still works once the page reads **Getting to know\nyou**. To get the Reflection, choose Screen history or Detailed screen history\n(Settings: Visual history or Adaptive visual history) in **Settings → Daily\nhistory → History mode**."],[658,"In **Files**, and the file rows in **All**, files whose names or text match\nappear at once; files that match what you meant follow a moment later. What\nyou type in the Concordance is searched on this Mac only."],[392,"In **Meetings**, press **Record** when a conversation starts, and tell the\nother people you're recording."],[100,"In **Settings → AI & summaries**, under **Files you include**, choose **Add\nFolder** to let Memory's **Ask** and the Concordance answer from your\ndocuments. They're indexed on this Mac with the same on-device search model as\neverything else: **Search model** in the same place chooses\n&#x2A;*EmbeddingGemma-2 (default)** or &#x2A;*Apple (previous)**. See\nSearch by meaning."],[469,"In **Settings → Meetings**:"],[145,"In **Start recording automatically** mode, when a matching meeting begins\nEverpaper opens a small countdown in the top-right corner. It shows the meeting\ntitle, the service name, and the attendee count, and counts down for 5 seconds."],[208,"In apps configured with JSON, the entry usually looks like this:"],[206,"In Everpaper, turn on Settings → **AI & summaries** → **Access from other AI\napps** → &#x2A;*Allow external agents (MCP)**."],[267,"In every mode except Screen history you can also turn on **Computer History**,\nwhich records clicks, shortcuts and the text you select or type. It's off until\nyou turn it on. See Computer History."],[108,"In Memory, the answer check can occasionally remove a correct detail or let\nan invented name through, so check the sources."],[57,"In setup's **Private summaries** step, Everpaper recommends one option for this\nMac and downloads nothing until you press **Prepare on this Mac**. (After\nsetup, the search model can still set itself up in the background; see\nSearch by meaning.) It decides in this order:"],[395,"In the **Access** step, on the **Screen and system audio** row, press\n**Allow access** (or **Open System Settings**). This adds Everpaper to the\nlist and opens **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Screen & System\nAudio Recording**."],[102,"In the same section, under **Access from other AI apps**, &#x2A;*Allow external\nagents (MCP)*&#x2A; (off by default) lets AI apps on this Mac read your people,\nfiles, links and connections, with secrets removed (names, email addresses and\nphone numbers are kept). They never get screenshots, and raw screen text needs\na second switch, &#x2A;*…including raw screen text (full-text search)**. What an\nagent reads is then handled by that app and the AI it uses, which may be a\ncloud service. **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal** doesn't apply to it,\nso turn this on only for apps you trust. To connect an app, see\nCommand line."],[207,"In your AI app's MCP settings, add a server that runs the command\n`/Applications/Everpaper.app/Contents/MacOS/everpaper-cli` with the\nargument `mcp`."],[846,"Includes b95's click fix. The old MOSS introduction card is gone."],[897,"Includes the b89 journal fixes and the b88 speech defaults. No new download;\npermissions and consent are unchanged."],[35,"inside Everpaper"],[1264,"Insights"],[192,"Inspect what Everpaper has learned about you (the Memory page\nin the app) and verify it against the same guard the app uses, without opening\nthe app."],[1297,"Installing a new beta"],[1320,"Issues on GitHub are public. Don't paste transcripts, screenshots or anything\nelse from your history. Describe the problem in words and include your build\ninfo: open **Settings → About** and press **Copy build info**, then paste it\ninto your report. It lists the version, build and where the app is installed.\nIf that location shows your Mac user name, remove it before posting. You can\nalso see the version in **Everpaper → About Everpaper**."],[1273,"It turns **Ready** once all four are met: **5 days**, **300 frames**, **60\ndistinct things** and **3 recurring threads**. Readiness follows the weakest of\nthe four, so a lopsided history (many frames but a single day) keeps warming\nuntil it fills out."],[334,"Join the beta and install"],[332,"Join the private beta, install Everpaper, walk through setup, and grant the permissions it needs."],[168,"JSON output"],[560,"JSON Web Tokens (JWTs)"],[530,"Keeping apps and sites out"],[33,"Key"],[182,"Key options:"],[14,"Known limits in this beta"],[837,"Known limits: evening entries still condense busy days. When several people\nshare your Mac's microphone, the live transcript shows everyone as \"Me\" until\nthe meeting ends."],[824,"Known limits: Gemma in Everpaper Native hasn't been tried on a real 16 GB Mac\nyet. The first request after the engine starts can take around half a\nminute, and after a restart the next request pauses for a few seconds.\nEvening entries still condense busy days and drop some meeting details. When\nseveral people share your Mac's microphone, the live transcript shows them\nall as \"Me\" until the meeting ends; the saved transcript separates them."],[802,"Known limits: preparing the search model takes a few minutes the first time\nand again after a macOS update; until then, search works as before. Bringing\nolder history up to date can take a while on a large library."],[812,"Known limits: timed screenshots cover your whole display, so a skipped site\nor a private window that is visible but not in front can still appear in\none. Without the extension, private-window detection depends on the window\ntitle and may miss some browsers or languages. A private window Everpaper\ncan't recognize is treated as a normal one, so add sensitive sites to\n**Skip sites** as well."],[111,"Let Everpaper watch your calendar to remind you before meetings or start recording for you. Everpaper only reads your calendar and never uploads it."],[468,"Let the other people know you're recording. If the speech model isn't on your\nMac yet, your first meeting downloads it; the audio is recorded either way."],[81,"LFM2 1.2B"],[78,"LFM2.5 2.6B"],[298,"Limitless and Rewind alternatives: what to use after Rewind stopped capturing."],[9,"llama.cpp engine (experimental)"],[163,"LLM"],[8,"LM Studio, Ollama, Nativ and your own endpoint"],[532,"Local AI sees the real thing"],[988,"Local AI setup gets a better brain, fitted to your Mac. The two builds are identical; b76 expires after 15 days, b77 never does."],[992,"Local AI setup now needs about 6 GB free instead of 10."],[693,"Mac launchers with clipboard history: how this launcher compares with Spotlight, Raycast and Alfred."],[1254,"macOS 14.2 or later."],[126,"macOS only asks for calendar permission once. If you decline, you can re-enable\nit later in **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Calendars**."],[84,"Macs with 48 GB or more also see a few larger models marked as not yet\navailable. They can't be chosen in this beta."],[190,"Manage the on-device speech models."],[7,"Managed oMLX"],[859,"Mark Moment, Stop & Review and Discard (with confirmation) stay available. A\nworkday you paused yourself still offers Resume and stays paused after the\nmeeting."],[1085,"Meet the Concordance — your memory, one keystroke away, from anywhere on your Mac."],[115,"Meeting eligibility"],[394,"Meeting recording and the screen history modes rely on the **Screen & System\nAudio Recording** permission. On recent macOS, granting it has one quirk worth\nknowing."],[1227,"Meeting recording now starts instantly."],[23,"Meeting recording, transcription and speaker labels"],[28,"Meeting to-dos in Today's focus"],[159,"Meetings"],[407,"Meetings"],[443,"Meetings"],[770,"Meetings"],[459,"Meetings is where Everpaper records, transcribes and summarizes your\nconversations. Recording and transcription happen on your Mac, and recordings\nare never uploaded. Each finished meeting is saved to your Library so you can\ncome back to it."],[910,"Meetings process faster with MOSS, your custom words help transcription, and\nword timing can be more precise."],[427,"Meetings records and transcribes your conversations on this Mac, with live\ncaptions and speaker labels. Start with **Record** on the Meetings page,\n**Record now** on Today, ⇧⌘M from any app, the menu-bar icon, or\n⌘K → **Begin meeting**. If you allow calendar access, Everpaper can\nremind you before meetings (by default, those with a video-call link), or start\nrecording automatically. With AI set up, each finished meeting gets an\nautomatic Summary. Meetings are saved to your Library with a title you can\nrename."],[962,"Meetings use MOSS by default, and the built-in intelligence path is easier to choose."],[162,"Memory (Second Brain)"],[408,"Memory (Second Brain)"],[1257,"Memory (Second Brain)"],[1291,"Memory has no page that lists the beliefs. They shape Ask's answers, Worth\nconfronting and How your picture has changed. To look one up, type in the\nIndex (⌘K): beliefs appear \"in the Commonplace Book\". You can also\nlist them from the command line. With a model connected, **AI\nrefinement of insights** (Settings → AI & summaries → Your memory & summaries,\non by default) lets the model tidy the list."],[429,"Memory learns from what you capture. **Ask** questions across your days,\nmeetings and files; see what it has learned about **You**; turn **Insights** into a draft email or follow-up event; explore\n**Connections** between people, projects and ideas; and **Export** everything\nas an Obsidian vault or a graph."],[27,"Memory's counts, Insights, Connections and the graph"],[297,"Microsoft Recall for Mac: how screen history compares with Windows Recall."],[253,"Mode"],[66,"Model"],[161,"Models"],[171,"Most commands accept `--json` for machine-readable output (for a transcript as\nJSON, use `meetings export <id> --format json --stdout`). Progress and status\nmessages go to standard error, so the JSON on standard output stays pipeable."],[670,"Move the selection with the up and down arrows, press Return to go,\nand Esc to dismiss — focus returns to the page you were on."],[361,"Moving through the pages changes nothing on its own. A setting is saved only\nwhen you pick something, and nothing downloads until you press **Prepare on\nthis Mac**. With **Reduce motion** on, pages fade instead of turning. With\n**Reduce transparency** on, or before macOS 26, pages show plain paper instead\nof glass."],[1210,"Much better speaker detection, plus new ways to fix up a meeting after it's\nrecorded."],[255,"Needs"],[16,"Needs an AI model"],[1293,"Needs an AI model: Ask, the Reflection, **Who you are**, and the explanation\nin a connection. Works without one: the counts, Insights, Connections and the\ngraph, email and event drafts, and Export. Connect a model under **Settings →\nAI & summaries → AI connection → AI option**. With a cloud provider, Memory\nanswers only after you turn on **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal**;\nyour questions and the excerpts they cite then go to that provider. The line\nat the top right of Memory shows which case applies (**Private model · Cloud\nAI off**, **Cloud model · Cloud AI on** or **No model connected**). See\nAI on your Mac."],[840,"Nemotron becomes the default for words and speaker labels on new setups."],[1313,"No check runs while a meeting is recording."],[1236,"No feature changes — everything in 1.0.0 below is unchanged."],[1242,"No feature changes — everything in 1.0.0 below is unchanged."],[109,"No new Episodes are written in this beta, so on a new install Memory's\nEpisodes tab stays empty."],[36,"none"],[139,"Not every calendar entry is a meeting. Under **Meeting eligibility**, choose how\nmuch an event needs before it counts:"],[264,"Nothing"],[996,"Nothing else changed in this build; it exists to get the fix out fast. The b74 improvements below are all included."],[548,"Nothing else. Everpaper has no analytics or telemetry. Recordings, screenshots\nand your calendar are never uploaded. Only text, such as screen text,\ntranscripts and meeting titles, can reach an AI you set up. The Everpaper\nbrowser extension talks only to Everpaper on this Mac."],[121,"Nothing happens until you grant calendar access. The **Calendar behavior**\nsetting controls reminders and auto-start; see\nWhat Everpaper keeps from your calendar\nfor the one thing that happens whatever the setting."],[364,"Nothing records automatically: you decide when to start and stop."],[330,"Obsidian canvas alternatives: what to open the graph export in."],[586,"off"],[589,"off"],[592,"off"],[595,"off"],[601,"off"],[610,"off"],[481,"Off by default; appears while live labels are on. A second on-device check of the live labels."],[598,"off; revoked when MCP is turned off"],[613,"on"],[619,"on"],[391,"On **Today**, press **Start workday journal** when you begin work."],[246,"On **Today**, press **Start workday journal**. In Screen history or Detailed\nscreen history, macOS first needs Screen Recording. Press **Open Settings** and\nturn Everpaper on under **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Screen &\nSystem Audio Recording*&#x2A;. If the switch doesn't respond, remove Everpaper with\n&#x2A;*−*&#x2A; and add it again with &#x2A;*+**, then relaunch Everpaper. Everpaper must be\nin your Applications folder for macOS to keep the grant. Activity only needs\nno permission."],[550,"On a new install, **Also delete expired text & recall data** (under &#x2A;*Manage\nsaved text…**) is on. In Daily history the same switch is called **Also delete\ntheir text & recall data**. After the same period, Everpaper also deletes that\nday's recognized text, search data, running notes and written entry. Some of\nthe day, such as the app timeline and the notes you typed yourself, can stay;\nto remove everything, delete the day. Turn the switch off to keep text and\nentries after the images go. If you upgraded from a build that already had\njournal data, it starts off and only images are deleted."],[286,"On a new install, **Also delete their text & recall data** is on. Past the\nsame limit, Everpaper also deletes that day's recognized text, search data,\nrunning notes and written entry. Some of the day, such as the app timeline and\nthe notes you typed in the review, can stay; to remove everything, delete the\nday. To keep your written days, turn that switch off or choose Forever before\nthe limit passes. Deleted days can't be restored."],[327,"On a new install, Everpaper deletes a day's written entry and recognized text\nafter your screenshot limit (30 days by default; see\nHow long your days are kept). Older\ndays then export with only their timeline and your notes. Export before then,\nor change that setting, if you want full notes for older days."],[1292,"On a new install, Everpaper deletes old material after 30 days. In **Settings →\nPrivacy → How long it stays**, **Keep screenshot images for** sets the window,\nand **Manage saved text… → Also delete expired text & recall data** is on. When\na capture passes that window, Everpaper deletes the image, its text, its search\nentries and the people, files and sites found in it, along with that day's\nrunning notes and written journal entry. The notes you typed in the end-of-day\nreview stay. Memory's Frames processed, Topics and Recurring threads,\nConnections and Ask then reach back only that far. To keep more, choose 90 days\nor Forever, or turn the switch off: images are still deleted, but the text and\nwhat Memory learned from it stay."],[508,"On a saved meeting's **Speakers** tab, click **Name this speaker** to name a\nvoice, or **Forget name** to undo. Everpaper fills in some names itself, and\nsays where each came from:"],[479,"On by default: speakers are labeled as you talk. When it's off, live captions say *Me* and *Others*, and speakers are separated after the call."],[222,"On failure in `--json` mode, the tool prints an error envelope instead of the\nnormal payload:"],[971,"On macOS 26 and earlier nothing appears; the card waits until the Mac can\nactually use it."],[684,"On macOS 27 it goes further:"],[53,"On macOS 27, Everpaper can run a model inside the app, with no server to\ninstall. Macs with 16 GB of memory or more get **Gemma 4 E4B**: about a 5.9 GB\ndownload, about 8.5 GB on disk (some tools show 7.9 GB), and about 16 GB of\nfree space needed while it installs. Macs with less get **Qwen3 4B**: about\n2 GB to download, 2.3 GB on disk and about 5.5 GB free while it installs."],[517,"On macOS 27, meetings are also indexed on this Mac by what was heard in them,\nso you can find one by its sound as well as its words. See\nSearch by meaning."],[203,"On macOS 27, you can check Everpaper Native without opening\nthe app:"],[277,"On save, your AI writes the day's entry: a first-person diary of what you did,\nincluding any meetings you recorded that day. It appears as **Day Summary** on\nthat day's page in Timeline. If no AI is set up, the day is still saved without\nan entry; once you set up AI, the day's page offers **Write up this day**. A\nday cut short by a crash or force-quit is written up automatically once\nEverpaper can."],[506,"On the **Overview** tab, **Generate** runs a template on the meeting: **Open\nThreads**, **Commitment Register**, **Decision Rationale**, **Meeting Memo**,\n**Risk Review**, **Follow-Up Letter** or **Perspective Matrix**."],[507,"On the Meetings page, the **After each meeting** card has the switch &#x2A;*Run\nmarked prompts automatically (sends the transcript to your AI provider)**. It's\noff by default. When it's on, every template marked Auto-run runs as soon as a\nmeeting finishes; **Meeting Memo** is marked out of the box. Use &#x2A;*Edit\nPrompts…** to add prompts, to pin, hide or mark them, or to reset the\nbuilt-ins."],[38,"on this Mac; Everpaper can install it for you"],[607,"on when AI is set up"],[524,"On-device by default"],[772,"On-device by default"],[1047,"One click, one brain: setup can now install your local AI for you."],[1033,"One model for meetings: words and voices together."],[565,"One-time passcodes (OTPs)"],[356,"Open **Everpaper** from Applications."],[1271,"Open **Memory** in the sidebar, or choose **Go → Second Brain**\n(⌘4). Memory has six tabs, on ⌥⌘1 to ⌥⌘6:\n**Ask**, **Episodes**, **You**, **Insights**, **Connections** and **Export**.\n⌥⌘← and ⌥⌘→ move to the previous or next tab. Memory\nopens on Ask."],[98,"Open **Settings → AI & summaries → AI connection** and pick an **AI option**.\nEnter a key, address or model if asked; **Refresh list** reads the models a\nserver offers. Then press **Save** and **Test Connection**. **Clear**\ndisconnects the current AI and removes its key. An asterisk beside the section\nmeans nothing is set up or the last test failed."],[122,"Open **Settings → Meetings** and scroll to **Calendar**, or click\n**Connect Calendar** in the **Upcoming** card on the Meetings page."],[399,"Open **Settings** (⌘,). **General → Mac permissions** shows the\nmicrophone, Screen & System Audio and Accessibility. **Meetings → During a\nmeeting → System audio route** shows whether meetings use the lighter System\nAudio Recording, with **Allow now** or **Check again**. **Meetings →\nCalendar** has calendar access. **Daily history** has the permissions for\nclicks and typed text. **AI & summaries → Files you include → Search model**\nchooses the search model. To walk through setup again, use **General → Setup &\ndata → Run setup again**; there, **Return to Everpaper** (or Esc)\ncloses it, and **Back** is ⌘\\[."],[633,"Open **Settings** (sidebar, or ⌘,). AI choices are in **AI &\nsummaries → AI connection**. What is saved, for how long, and what is shared\nare in **Privacy**."],[1305,"Open Everpaper again. Your history, settings and models stay where they\nwere."],[29,"Open loops and cooling threads, related moments, email and event drafts, Export"],[355,"Open the disk image and drag **Everpaper** onto the **Applications** folder\nshortcut."],[691,"Open the meeting, choose the **Ask** tab and type in &#x2A;*Ask this meeting…**.\nWhile a meeting is still recording, use &#x2A;*Ask about what’s been said…** under\nthe live transcript. A single finished day has &#x2A;*Ask this day…** at the foot of\nits page."],[1304,"Open the new disk image from your latest link and drag **Everpaper** into\n**Applications**. Choose **Replace**."],[669,"Open with an empty query and the Index shows a table of contents instead: the\nParts of your book (Today, Meetings, Timeline, Library, Memory, Settings), your\nthree most recent days and meetings, and common commands."],[31,"Option"],[39,"optional"],[45,"optional"],[42,"optional (Ollama: none)"],[637,"Optionally set a **base URL** (for OpenAI-compatible endpoints this is\nrequired) and a **model**, or use **Refresh list** to pull available models."],[551,"Original typed text from Computer History expires after 48 hours. Meeting\nrecordings are kept until you delete them."],[205,"Other AI apps can read from your journal through the Model Context Protocol\n(MCP). It's off until you turn it on:"],[166,"Other commands"],[198,"Other useful options:"],[539,"Out of the box, everything happens on your machine:"],[223,"Parsing errors (exit 64) are not wrapped in the envelope."],[221,"Pass `--json` to get stable, pretty-printed JSON with sorted keys and ISO-8601\ndates. Status text is kept off standard output, so you can pipe results\nstraight into other tools:"],[237,"Pausing capture"],[563,"Payment card numbers"],[252,"Pick a mode in **Settings → Daily history → History mode**. Setup uses slightly\ndifferent names; both are shown here."],[635,"Pick a provider from **AI option** (or **None** to disconnect)."],[30,"Pick one in **Settings** (⌘,) → **AI & summaries** → **AI connection** → **AI option**."],[473,"Pick the input, and use **Test input** to check it."],[983,"Pick your local AI model — or let Everpaper pick for your Mac."],[1175,"Plus the usual polish: standardized section headings and spacing, a centered empty state on Meetings, and sharper accessibility labels."],[1168,"Plus: the Apple Notes Automation entitlement for one-click todo export, LLM-assisted phrasing of suggested to-dos when a provider is connected, meeting action items flowing into the Today's Focus rail, and dozens of accessibility improvements."],[106,"Preparing the search model takes a few minutes the first time and again\nafter a macOS update; until then, search works as before. Screenshot and\nmeeting-audio search need macOS 27."],[280,"Press **Pause** to stop capture for a while. You can note why with a quick\nreason — **Meeting**, **Break**, **Lunch**, **Personal** — or type your own.\nPress **Resume** to start again. The pause and its reason are recorded, so a\nfinished day shows when and why capture stopped. The Everpaper menu-bar icon\nhas **Pause Journal** and **Resume Journal** too."],[638,"Press **Save**, then **Test Connection**."],[425,"Press **Start workday journal** on Today when you begin work. Nothing is\ncaptured until you do. While it runs, Everpaper keeps a private history in the\nmode you chose (Screen history, Detailed screen history or Activity only).\nPress **Stop & Review** and it writes up your day. Finished days live in\n**Timeline**, one per page: turn with the arrow keys, use **Ask the book**\n(⌘F) to search across every day, or &#x2A;*Ask this day…** on one page.\nOn Today, &#x2A;*Ask your memory…** asks across everything, and ⇧⌘A\ntakes you there from any page."],[276,"Press **Stop & Review** (⌘R). Everpaper finishes its notes, then\nopens **Here's the day** on Today. Read the running notes, answer any\nquestions, add notes for the day, and press **Save Journal**. (**Discard**\ndeletes the whole day — its observations, screenshots and your answers — and\ncan't be undone.)"],[661,"Press ⌘K anywhere in Everpaper to open the Index. It is both a\ntable of contents and a search box for the whole book."],[424,"Press ⌘K for the Index to jump to any day, meeting, setting or\ncommand. On Timeline, the arrow keys turn between days. The red ribbon at the\ntop right of every other page, or ⌘T, brings you back to Today. From\nany app, ⌥Space opens the Concordance, a quick find-and-ask panel."],[293,"Press ⌘L for the **Day Index** — a chronological overview of every\ncaptured day, newest first. Each row shows the day's title, date, frame count,\nand a thumbnail of its most recent capture. Use ↑/↓ to\nmove and Return to turn straight to a day. A density strip at the\ntop shows how many days you've captured and the span of your history."],[657,"Press ⌥Space in any app to open the Concordance, a small floating\npanel. Choose **Find** to jump to a day, a meeting or an indexed file, or\n**Ask** to get an answer about anything you've captured. Narrow it with\n**All**, **Days**, **Meetings** or **Files** (⌘1–⌘4)."],[410,"Privacy"],[457,"Privacy"],[522,"Privacy"],[605,"Privacy → Voices, storage & journal controls"],[614,"Privacy → What gets saved"],[617,"Privacy → What gets saved"],[559,"Private keys"],[1014,"Progress you can see on every engine, and a beta that knows when to stop."],[649,"Quick filters"],[72,"Qwen3.5 4B"],[75,"Qwen3.5 9B"],[1042,"Raw now means raw for meetings too."],[428,"Read more about Meetings →"],[430,"Read more about Memory →"],[426,"Read more about the Day Journal →"],[643,"Recall"],[656,"Recall is how you ask your book a question and get a real answer back — built\nfrom what you actually saw and recorded, not from the open web. You can ask\nfrom four places. **The Concordance** (⌥Space, from any app) finds\nor asks across days, meetings and indexed files. **The Index** (⌘K)\njumps to a page. &#x2A;*Ask your memory…** on Today searches anything you saw.\n**Memory → Ask** (⌥⌘1) is for longer conversations. Single days and\nmeetings have their own Ask fields."],[692,"Recall reads your own on-device book. Search and the card results stay\nentirely on your Mac. Search by meaning uses the on-device search model, not\nyour AI provider, so your searches are never sent online to be understood.\nThe one exception is an **External embeddings provider**, an advanced option\nyou would have set yourself: files, and questions asked about them, then also\ngo through it. The Concordance's type-ahead never uses it. See\nFiles and other AI apps.\nThe streamed prose answer uses the AI you set up in\n**Settings → AI & summaries**; an on-device provider keeps that on your Mac\ntoo. See AI on your Mac."],[444,"Record and transcribe meetings on your Mac: a live panel with notes, captions and Ask, speaker names, and an automatic summary."],[1245,"Record and transcribe meetings on-device. Start one from the Record tile, the\nmenu bar, the Index (**Begin minutes**), or the ⇧⌘M hotkey. Hindsight\ncan watch your calendar and offer to begin a recording when a meeting starts,\nrecognizing Google Meet, Zoom, and Microsoft Teams links."],[516,"Recording and transcription run entirely on your Mac, and recordings are never\nuploaded. AI features (live Ask, the As we go digest, the automatic Summary,\ntemplates, marked prompts and to-do extraction) use the AI provider you set\nup. A provider on your Mac keeps everything local. A cloud provider receives\nmeeting text only after you turn on **Allow cloud AI to analyze your journal**\nin **Settings → AI & summaries**. Before anything is sent, secrets such as API\nkeys and card numbers are removed, and email addresses, phone numbers and names\nit detects are replaced. See AI on your Mac."],[518,"Recording laws differ by place. Tell people when you're recording them."],[1020,"Recordings that finish themselves, and a wait you can w